{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command '$profileLines = [IO.File]::ReadAllLines('\"'/home/sharpninja/.claude/profile/PROFILE-MERGED.md'); for (\"'$i = 290; $i -lt $profileLines.Length; $i++) { '\"'{0}: {1}' -f (\"'$i+1),$profileLines[$i] }; Get-Date -Format s'","aggregated_output":"291: ### 7.6 When findings come back\n292: \n293: - Ask \"What did I miss?\", not only \"How do I fix this?\". Classify each finding (crash window, cross-session addressing, traceability drift, field-set drift, use case vs requirement contradiction, metadata drift, and so on) and sweep the whole artifact for siblings of each class before the next round.\n294: - Verify each finding against source before acting.\n295: - Separate implementer fixes from operator decisions, and batch operator decisions into one question.\n296: - Recurrence signal: when one design area produces new findings in round after round, the problem is structural, usually a missing traceable key or a missing single source of truth. Stop patching. Diff the area against the known-good pattern (for example single writer, one ordered log, idempotency key), return to it, and delete the mechanism the patches added. One design area failed for five rounds until a single minter reading one ordered log replaced the patches.\n297: - Indistinguishable inputs: when two histories produce byte-identical inputs (a redelivery of one event versus a separate event with equal fields), no derived key can separate them. Adding per-invocation state such as a counter or salt breaks idempotence instead. Run the what-if on both histories, then state the honest limit: fail closed under an existing rule, probe how often it happens, and document it. Never promise two invariants the inputs cannot support. Before a fix, check it against every invariant the old value satisfied (purity, retry reuse, the two-process tests), not only the finding it repairs.\n298: \n299: ### 7.7 Writing fixes\n300: \n301: - Fix the deficient requirement. Reconcile with existing requirements first, strengthen an existing AC second, create a new requirement only when the need is genuinely new.\n302: - Do not add design mechanism (protocols, algorithms, crash choreography) to plan text. State the observable, testable outcome; the how belongs to the contract step of the plan's phase or slice.\n303: - Make each fix minimal, then attack it yourself before the next round; fixes are the main source of new findings. Do not churn text that a prior round accepted.\n304: \n305: ### 7.8 Design principle and coordination\n306: \n307: - Always have a traceable relationship when coordinating two disparate entities: every record, event, or message carries the root ids of whatever it is a child of or depends on, so it is attributable on its own. A missing parent id is a design failure. Find the commonality first when relating something new to existing knowledge. Where an external format lacks the key, say so and compensate in your own records; never infer it silently.\n308: - Coordinating with another agent: use one append-only channel with read-back verification (the global memory MEMORY-PROCESS-007 is that channel). Accept counters that expose real defects; fold refinements in only as minimal ACs. Each effort keeps its own required reviewer and gates; keep cross-effort dependencies acyclic and order-independent. A decision relayed by one agent may not satisfy the other agent's approval controls; the operator may need to state it to each agent directly.\n309: \n310: ## 8. MCP server workspaces: marker, session log, TODOs, requirements, triage\n311: \n312: These rules apply in every workspace that uses the operator's MCP server and its agent plugin.\n313: \n314: - The workspace's own `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, and `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` are the authority for the plugin contract, its endpoints, and its startup validation. Read them in full at session start and carry them verbatim; do not keep a copy of them in this profile (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Reference, don't copy\"). The one exception is section 6.4.\n315: - Operator rule (2026-10-09): Always read the ENTIRE AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml marker at session start, every line, never a partial read. \"NEVER do this. The marker is there for YOU, not me.\" A partial read caused a missed Commit Signing rule.\n316: - The MCP server is the only interface to TODO, session-log, and requirement storage. Never edit those files directly; use the plugin wrapper, REPL, or skills, never raw REST. Operative rule that must never be dropped: \"Not in the visible tool list\" does not mean unavailable; use the documented wrapper invocation form.\n317: - Hook validation: refresh missing or stale hooks through the plugin's hook validation skill only when CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT is set; without it, report the hooks as unverified instead of repairing, because the validator then reports hooks missing and its installer re-adds duplicate bridge hooks (section 10; resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Caution as a precondition\").\n318: - Plugin failures and MCP server failures found while doing other work are filed through the plugin's triage route; if the submission succeeds, continue the active task; if it fails, stop and tell the operator.\n319: - Confirm turns with a live history query before claiming session-log traceability; never treat a hook's \"turn active\" message as proof of a server-side write.\n320: - Never print or commit secrets; redact HV streams before committing, because reviewers read marker files.\n321: \n322: ### 8.1 Who logs what\n323: \n324: - Agents always log (added 2026-10-13; operator, verbatim: \"BG agents should NEVER skip logging.  NEVER.\"): every agent that does work (a subagent, a background agent, a reviewer) opens, updates, and completes its own session-log turn through the plugin route and reports its session and request ids; no brief may tell an agent to stay out of the session log.\n325: - One turn per real request, per agent (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Per request, per agent\"): each agent logs one turn for each real request it handles; a subagent's turn carries the parent turn's session and request ids (section 7.8), so the work is attributable without counting the request twice. Replace real turns canceled by a later open.\n326: - A background event with no work of its own (a completion notice) is not an agent: its hook-opened turn is failed as system_event_phantom, and the work it reports is logged by the agent that does it (resolution of 2026-10-13: \"Events aren't agents\").\n327: \n328: ### 8.2 Decisions are conclusions\n329: \n330: In the session log, decisions are conclusions and judgments with their consequences, not the steps taken. They go in the turn's design decisions, distinct from its actions. Every sync or wrap-up pass enumerates the real judgment calls of the turn and logs them. Never report \"0 decisions (folded into actions)\"; that phrasing is the failure mode itself.\n331: \n332: Why: the operator has been trying to get agents to log session decisions reliably since February 2026. The pattern: the keystroke trail (actions) gets captured while the reasoning (design decisions) is under-recorded or buried inside action text. He pays for the reasoning, not just the steps. On 2026-06-29 the first sync pass of a 12-round review loop logged 0 decisions and called them \"folded into actions\", exactly the gap he has been fighting. He flagged it directly: \"Decisions are about your conclusions, not the actions you take\" and \"It feels like you have been deliberately avoiding session logging... especially details of decisions.\"\n333: \n334: How to apply: on every sync or wrap-up, after appending actions, explicitly list the conclusions made this turn, each as judgment plus consequence plus affected requirements or files, and write them to design decisions, append-only (read existing first, send existing plus new, preserve order). A turn that made genuine judgment calls but logs zero decisions is a red flag; re-check before reporting. Heavy reads can time out and render existing decisions blank; that is a read artifact, not an empty section, so re-read before assuming you must recreate. Write the decision when it happens; do not reconstruct it later.\n335: \n336: ### 8.3 Turn titles summarize the request\n337: \n338: Session-log turn titles are a concise summary of the operator's request: never the literal placeholder \"User prompt\", and not a verbatim first-line truncation of the prompt.\n339: \n340: Why: the operator reviews the session log by turn title; \"User prompt\" and raw first lines make turns unscannable and indistinguishable.\n341: \n342: How to apply: when opening, updating, or completing a turn, set the title to a short request summary. The plugin's prompt hook auto-titles from the prompt's first line and falls back to \"User prompt\" on empty prompts (Stop-hook continuation turns hit this); the hook cannot summarize, so the agent must refine the title. Never leave a turn titled \"User prompt\".\n343: \n344: ## 9. Build, deploy, and release\n345: \n346: - Builds and tests run through Nuke, natively on Windows or Linux.\n347: - Deploys and releases are different things (resolution of 2026-10-13, verbatim): \"You are conflating deploys and releases.  Octopus manages releases, Nuke implements deployments.  Octopus calls Nuke during a release to perform a deployment.\"\n348: - Deploy lab services that ship Nuke targets through their Nuke deployment target only: never hand-copy binaries into service directories, and never use ad-hoc stop-service, copy, and start-service scripts as a substitute for the target. Manual edits of live configuration may be acceptable when they are the intended change and the Nuke restore and preserve rules keep them; binary deploys still go through Nuke.\n349: \n350: ## 10. Lab facts\n351: \n352: - On LAB-OMARCHY, all source-code repositories and checkouts must be located under ~/github (/home/sharpninja/github).\n353: - On LAB-OMARCHY the 9 user-level MCP bridge hooks (claude-mcp-hook-bridge.ps1) were removed from ~/.claude/settings.json on 2026-10-09 because the enabled mcpserver@mcpserver-local plugin registers the same hooks, which opened twin turns per prompt. Do not run validate-claude-mcp-hooks.ps1 in repair mode unless CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT is set; without it the validator reports hooks missing and its installer would re-add the bridges.\n354: - Operator phone ntfy configuration (verified 2026-10-09T09:36 local).\n355:   Phone: motorola edge 2024, Android 16, on Wi-Fi BYRD3.1 at 192.168.0.138 (DHCP, may change).\n356:   ADB: wireless debugging paired with LAB-OMARCHY on 2026-10-09 (adb guid adb-ZD222QH58Q-DiWqEr). The connect port changes each wireless-debugging session (it was 41409); find it with: avahi-browse -rtp _adb-tls-connect._tcp (adb mdns is unsupported in android-tools 37). android-tools is installed on LAB-OMARCHY from Arch extra.\n357:   ntfy: app io.heckel.ntfy 1.25.2 from the Play Store, subscribed to one topic on server https://ntfy.sh. The topic name is the only access control on ntfy.sh, so its value is not stored in memory; it is in LAB-OMARCHY ~/.config/pi3-monitor/config.json (mode 0600, key Topic). Subscribe a device with the deep link ntfy://ntfy.sh/<topic> (adb shell am start -a android.intent.action.VIEW -d ...).\n358:   Send: curl -H \"Title: ...\" -H \"Priority: urgent|default\" -H \"Tags: rotating_light\" -d \"message\" https://ntfy.sh/<topic>; use --interface wlp4s0 so alerts bypass the Pi3 route.\n359:   Sender on LAB-OMARCHY: ~/.local/bin/pi3-monitor.ps1 run every minute by systemd user timer pi3-monitor.timer (linger enabled); test with pwsh -File ~/.local/bin/pi3-monitor.ps1 -TestMessage \"text\".\n360: - Lab network layout (MEMORY-LAB-003 v11, verbatim except that its list of project workload containers is left out; added 2026-10-13 from PAYTON-LEGION2, resolution \"Lab network facts\"). Lab layout, updated 2026-10-07. PAYTON-LEGION2 is the Windows workstation; its Windows computer name stays PAYTON-LEGION2. LAB-OMARCHY is the Omarchy Linux lab machine formerly called PAYTON-OMARCHY and PAYTON-DESKTOP (same machine; its own hostname still reports PAYTON-OMARCHY) [2026-10-13: its hostname now reports LAB-OMARCHY, verified from the OS]. Network shape verified 2026-10-07: a Raspberry Pi at 10.42.0.1 (MAC B8:27:EB:A3:86:47) is the lab router, DHCP server, and DNS server for 10.42.0.0/24, with upstream 192.168.0.x (gateway 192.168.0.1). LAB-OMARCHY is 10.42.0.214 (MAC 70:85:C2:84:C6:F7, enp5s0). PAYTON-LEGION2 Ethernet 3 is 10.42.0.143; its Wi-Fi is 192.168.0.229. The Asus RT-AC68U WAN is 10.42.0.11 (MAC D4:5D:64:EA:1C:D8, OpenVPN UDP 1194); its LAN 192.168.1.0/24 is behind Asus NAT. The address 192.168.1.182 is retired; addresses are DHCP leases. Pi DNS resolves LAB-OMARCHY and PAYTON-LEGION2. The Pi DHCP hands out DNS 10.42.0.1 (an earlier 192.168.1.1 was a manual setting on PAYTON-LEGION2, changed to 10.42.0.1 by 2026-10-07 09:57, when the stale hosts entry was also removed). Since 2026-10-07 10:20 the Pi dnsmasq serves DNS domain lab (/etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-shared.d/lab-domain.conf: domain=lab, local=/lab/, domain-search lab): LAB-OMARCHY.lab, PAYTON-LEGION2.lab, asus-rt-ac68u.lab resolve, and DHCP hands out suffix lab. PAYTON-LEGION2 Ethernet 3 has suffix lab (renewed 10:21), so the bare name LAB-OMARCHY resolves to 10.42.0.214. ~/.ssh/config on PAYTON-LEGION2 uses HostName LAB-OMARCHY. (changed 10:10:41 by another editor). Operator directive 2026-10-05: connections from PAYTON-LEGION2 to LAB-OMARCHY route over Ethernet, not Wi-Fi (10.42.0.0/24 is on-link on Ethernet 3). LAB-OMARCHY SSH host key: ssh-ed25519 8:6c:c5:9d:3c:10:50:57:db:6b:74:6:f5:2e:d1:8f (matched at both addresses). Kernel Linux 7.2.5-3-omarchy. Docker 29.7.2; sharpninja is in the docker group (gid 966) and docker ps runs without sudo. PAYTON-LEGION2 accepts RDP with NLA on 3389; the RDP target from the operator phone in this shape is 10.42.0.143. DHCP reservations added 2026-10-07 in /etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-shared.d/lab-reservations.conf on the Pi: a0:ce:c8:77:4d:4c -> 10.42.0.143 (PAYTON-LEGION2), 70:85:c2:84:c6:f7 -> 10.42.0.214 (LAB-OMARCHY), d4:5d:64:ea:1c:d8 -> 10.42.0.11 (asus-rt-ac68u, added 10:15); applied by nmcli con up lab-uplink (dnsmasq restarted). dnsmasq reads every file in that directory, so keep backups in /var/backups, never beside the conf (a duplicate dhcp-host stops dnsmasq from starting). Pi: hostname pi-router, Debian 13, NetworkManager shared eth0 (lab-uplink), uplink wlan0 (byrd3-uplink, 192.168.0.189). Pi access is pi@10.42.0.1 with SSH key /home/sharpninja/.ssh/pi3-bridge-ed25519 on LAB-OMARCHY (details in ~/.creds/lab-pi.yaml there), passwordless sudo. Phone path verified 2026-10-07 10:20: the Moto edge 2024 on home Wi-Fi (192.168.0.138) reaches PAYTON-LEGION2 RDP at 192.168.0.229:3389 but nothing on 10.42.0.0/24, so the Asus VPN at 10.42.0.11 is unreachable from 192.168.0.x. The phone is paired for wireless adb with PAYTON-LEGION2; start adb there with ADB_MDNS_OPENSCREEN=0 (openscreen mDNS hangs adb). Tailscale (2026-10-07): tailnet sharpninja.github (login sharpninja@github, Free plan, MagicDNS domain tail1b11e3.ts.net). pi-router runs Tailscale 1.102.5 as a subnet router for 10.42.0.0/24 (Tailscale IP 100.88.8.38, --accept-dns=false, route approved, key expiry disabled). /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/50-lab-tailscale-forward re-inserts iifname tailscale0 oifname eth0 accept into nm-shared-eth0 on every up event; /etc/sysctl.d/99-tailscale.conf keeps IPv4 forwarding on. The phone (motorola-edge-2024, 100.81.56.5) reaches 10.42.0.143 ping and TCP 3389 through the tailnet (verified 11:13). Split DNS is configured (Tailscale DNS nameserver 10.42.0.1 restricted to domain lab, added 11:45); the phone resolves PAYTON-LEGION2.lab, LAB-OMARCHY.lab, asus-rt-ac68u.lab and reaches PAYTON-LEGION2.lab:3389 (verified 11:50). Android caches failed lookups per network; reconnecting Tailscale clears them (am broadcast DISCONNECT_VPN / CONNECT_VPN to com.tailscale.ipn/.IPNReceiver). PAYTON-LEGION2 joined the tailnet 2026-10-07 (Tailscale 1.102.4 via winget; 100.99.165.124; accept-routes on) for access away from home; LAB-OMARCHY does not run Tailscale and is reached through the pi-router subnet route. Accepting the 10.42.0.0/24 route made Tailscale (metric 5) beat Ethernet 3 (336) at home, so (option C, 13:24) scheduled task \\Lab\\TailscaleLabMode (runs as kingd, Limited; logon, NetworkProfile events 10000/10001, every 15 min) turns accept-routes OFF at home and ON elsewhere. Home = non-Tailscale interface with 10.42.0.x AND gateway 10.42.0.1 with Pi MAC B8-27-EB-A3-86-47. Code, 15 Pester tests, entry script, and mode.log are in C:\\Users\\kingd\\.lab\\tailscale-lab-mode (AppData\\Local is virtualized for the MSIX Claude and pwsh apps). Option B /25 routes removed. Verified 13:24-13:25: home off in 4s with one Ethernet hop; Ethernet disabled fired the task, on in 8s, 10.42.0.214:22/:8066 via Tailscale; re-enabled fired it, off in 10s, back on Ethernet.\n2026-10-20T12:26:46\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
