ked-out commit of `native/vice`: both `cc67418cba58e71d8e70fd353e9de47199ad2075` (`git submodule status`, 10:50). `.gitmodules` carries `ignore = dirty`, which hides the submodule's dirtiness from the superproject's status but changes nothing inside the submodule. The submodule's git dir is `.git/modules/native/vice`, so its local exclude file is `.git/modules/native/vice/info/exclude` (`git rev-parse --git-path`), a machine-local, uncommitted file.\n8: - LAB-OMARCHY: 8 tracked modifications (`vice/src/c64/c64cpusc.c`, `drive/drive-snapshot.c`, `drive/drivecpu.c`, `drive/drivecpu65c02.c`, `mainc64cpu.c`, `resid/filter8580new.h`, `resid/sid.h`, `sid/resid.cc`; 222 insertions, 8 deletions) and 135 untracked entries (autotools output: the `.in` files, `configure`, `compile`, `depcomp`, `missing`, `ylwrap`, copied `COPYING` and `INSTALL`; `git status --short`, 10:50; the Phase A classification found no untracked code file here).\n9: - PAYTON-LEGION2 (Phase 0 receipt `docs/receipts/native-vice/20261004T090000-0500-preexisting-delta/`, 2026-10-04): 12 tracked modifications (the 8 above plus `mainviccpu.c`, `vic20/vic20cpu.c`, `vic20/vic20sound.c`, `vic20/vic20sound.h`; 399 insertions, 10 deletions), 2,527 untracked entries (745 `.o`, 59 `.a`, 2 `.exe`, 1,432 `.po`, 106 `Makefile`, 107 `.in`, generated headers, `mon_lex.c`, `mon_parse.c`, `psiddrv.h`) and one injected source file, `vice/src/vice-shim-runtime.h`, with `.vice-shim-configure-flags`. The receipt holds the full tracked patch (SHA256 `90604E39...`, reverse-apply validated against the dirty checkout on Oct 4), the preserved header (`5450FDA8...`) and the untracked inventory. The Oct 4 patch is a superset of today's Linux delta; whether LEGION2's tracked delta is still byte-identical to the Oct 4 patch is measured in step D0, not assumed.\n10: - What the tracked modifications do (read from `git -C native/vice diff`, 10:50): the hosted `CLK_INC` in `c64cpusc.c` and `mainc64cpu.c` exports the CPU register file to the shim after each cycle, pauses or stops at the cycle boundary (`vice_shim_cycle_checkpoint`), and re-imports registers; `mainc64cpu.c` also adopts a host-provided register file at bootstrap (`vice_shim_take_bootstrap_maincpu`) instead of the power-on reset; `mainviccpu.c` and `vic20cpu.c` do the same for xvic; `drive-snapshot.c`, `drivecpu.c` and `drivecpu65c02.c` relax or extend snapshot module handling; `resid/*` and `sid/resid.cc` add SID filter probes (`native/patches/s9-filter-probe.patch`); `vic20sound.c/.h` add VIC sound probes (Windows only). The shim DLL built from this delta (`native/vice_x64.dll`, P/Invoked by `tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/ViceNativeBridge.cs`) is what the legacy parity suites consume.\n11: - Build paths today: `native/Vice.Native.proj` runs `build-vice-shim.ps1` on Windows before the TestHarness build and declares `vice\\vice\\src\\**` as inputs (writes `vice_x64.dll` under `native/`, patches and configures inside `native/vice`); the Linux `build-vice-shim.sh` and `build-vice-shim-xvic.sh` build inside `native/vice`. The Nuke `Test` target excludes `Determinism`, `AiReview`, `ParityPending`, `ParityLegacy` and `Integration`; no `BuildViceNative` target exists in `build/*.cs` (grep, 10:50). `ViceSharp.slnx` lists `AiReview`, `Library`, `Heads` and `Library.IntegrationTests` and the TestHarness; none of the three native-safe projects (`ViceSharp.NativeClockProbe.Tests`, `ViceSharp.OracleContract.Tests`, `ViceSharp.VsfCompatibility.Tests`) is in the solution or the Nuke `Test` target.\n12: - Already in place from Phase C: `native/build-vice-observer.ps1` builds the stock VICE objects from the hash-verified copy of the materialization and relinks `x64sc-observer` and `xvic-observer` with external translation units only, through MSYS2 on Windows, with every generated file under `artifacts/immutable-vice/<sha>/` (gitignored) and the materialization re-hashed unchanged; proven on both hosts (receipts `20261020T081800-phase-c-step3-s2b-observer-build-linux/`, LEGION2's `receipts-windows`). The observer binaries carry the per-cycle gate, the bus observations and the `VICE_OBS` records that Phase E consumes.\n13: \n14: ## 2. Classification of the tracked modifications and the injected header (step 3, decided here)\n15: \n16: Observation, per file, with the disposition this plan proposes:\n17: \n18: 1. Hosted `CLK_INC` pause/stop checkpoint (`c64cpusc.c`, `mainc64cpu.c`, `mainviccpu.c`, `vic20cpu.c`): observation and control logic. Rehomed: the clock gate (`clock-gate.c`, `gate-observer.c`, `clock-observer.c`, `clock-observer-x64sc.c`) pauses at every cycle boundary through `--wrap=vic_cycle` and `--wrap=vicii_cycle` without a source change. Disposition: reject from the oracle.\n19: 2. Per-cycle register export and re-import (`EXPORT_REGISTERS()` / `IMPORT_REGISTERS()` in the same macro): observation of CPU-internal state. The registers are locals of the interpreter loop (`6510core.c`), unreachable by link-time wrapping; the observer reports the bus instead (address, data, kind, clock, interrupt mask, BA, stolen, per half-cycle). Disposition: reject from the oracle; Phase E item 1 moves the C64 comparison from registers to the observer field set (already the handoff's wording), and register parity at instruction boundaries, if still wanted, is a Phase E question for Payton, not a reason to keep the patch.\n20: 3. Bootstrap from a host-provided register file (`vice_shim_take_bootstrap_maincpu`): resume-from-host-state. Disposition: reject from the oracle; resume is the VSF path (`PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001`, native `.vsf` as the canonical snapshot), where the external `MAINCPU` adapter (S5) is the only compatibility path.\n21: 4. Snapshot module relaxations (`drive-snapshot.c`, `drivecpu.c`, `drivecpu65c02.c`): legacy snapshot normalization. Disposition: already rehomed in the external VSF adapter (199 green cases); reject from the oracle. Step D0 diffs the three hunks against the adapter's covered cases and lists any hunk the adapter does not cover as a finding before the revert.\n22: 5. SID and VIC sound probes (`resid/filter8580new.h`, `resid/sid.h`, `sid/resid.cc`, `vic20sound.c/.h`; `native/patches/s9-filter-probe.patch`): observation of chip internals for the S9 filter work. Disposition: reject from the oracle; the patch files under `native/patches/` stay as evidence (never applied); whether an external probe is wanted later is a separate requirement, planned then.\n23: 6. Injected `vice/src/vice-shim-runtime.h` and `.vice-shim-configure-flags` (LEGION2 only): injected source and configuration for the shim. Disposition: generated or injected, preserved in the Phase 0 receipt; excluded, not deleted (D4), until Payton clears deletion.\n24: \n25: Consequence (inference, stated as such): nothing in the delta needs to migrate into managed ViceSharp code for Phase D to exit; every observation need is served by the wrappers or by the VSF adapter, and every control need by the gate. If step D0 finds a hunk that is neither, it becomes a named blocker and a question to Payton, not a silent migration.\n26: \n27: ## 3. Decisions for Payton (answered 2026-10-20T10:57 through the session question; verbatim option labels)\n28: \n29: - R1: \"B: .gitmodules ignore = untracked only\". Consequence: `.gitmodules` changes `ignore = dirty` to `ignore = untracked`; the superproject's status shows the submodule clean once the tracked delta is reverted while untracked build products remain inside it; no exclude file is written. The immutable Phase 3 exit criterion \"`status --porcelain=v1 -uall` returns no entries\" is therefore amended (section 4, D4 and Exit): the tracked diff is empty, and the submodule's untracked entries are exactly the classified generated or injected inventory recorded in the D0 receipt, none new, none deleted. I had flagged that B leaves the submodule's own status dirty; Payton chose it anyway, so it stands as his decision.\n30: - R2 and R3: \"A: retire it now\". The shi