cile each to its VICE-derived AC first, never by weakening assertions. `FR-CPU-004` criteria depend on decision D2.\n124: 3. `PLAN-VICBA-001`: port VICE's stateful 3-cycle BA to AEC `prefetch_cycles` model (`viciisc/vicii-cycle.c`), model-aware PAL/NTSC, with `RasterBarLockstepTests` and `X64ScVariantLockstepTests.FirstTenProfileFramesMatch` green together (`TR-CYCLE-001` AC2).\n125: 4. If the per-instruction phase flags in `Mos6502.cs` cannot satisfy the full-field comparison, execute locked decision 7 of the goal plan: one per-cycle engine whose phases are the VICE macros in `6510dtvcore.c`, 256 opcodes, decimal ADC/SBC, indirect JMP page wrap, JAM; pin each closed mismatch in `CpuVisiblePcTimingTests`. This is the architectural break the plan already approved; it still gets its own decision-complete slice plan before code.\n126: 5. Restored-checkpoint lockstep: VSF Slices 2 and 3 (production C64 bridge, full pipeline and media state transfer, `C64ViceSnapshotLockstepTests.RestoredCheckpoint_MatchesX64ScFor100000Cycles`). Requires decision D5.\n127: \n128: Exit: from-reset 100,000-cycle full-field lockstep green on every C64 profile in `X64ScVariantLockstepTests`; restored-checkpoint 100,000-cycle lockstep green on the supplied fixture (`tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/Fixtures/Vsf/ready-c64sc-truedrive.vsf`, SHA256 `5C284F5CF0A1BC7E01A2E4117A74158B7A9D60485F5626448F4B7655C0E23C19`); Lorenz suite and the VICE cycle-exact test programs match x64sc (`TR-CYCLE-001` AC5, AC6) with named workloads added to a C64 oracle manifest beside the VIC-20 one; full suite green; HV AGREE.\n129: \n130: ### Phase F: VIC-20 half-cycle lockstep (Grok plan Slice 3c, immutable Phase 4)\n131: \n132: Entry: Phase E accepted. The six repair pairs are the goal plan's locked order; each is a mock-first slice, its own diagnostic filter, the full suite, C64 pins green, and HV.\n133: \n134: 1. Foundations: `SystemClock`, VIC-20 memory map and banking, bus. No instruction semantics.\n135: 2. CPU engine on the VIC-20 (`vic20cpu.c` fast opcode-fetch path, `maincpu_steal_cycles` bookkeeping observed through the wrapper); pin mismatches in `CpuVisiblePcTimingTests`.\n136: 3. Machine glue (`PLAN-ARCHVIC20-001`: RAM expansion variants, cartridge port, IEC wiring).\n137: 4. VIC-I (6560/6561), including `FR-VIC20-001` criteria (today marked 6 of 6 satisfied while pending; re-prove them here).\n138: 5. VIA (shared chip, VIC-20 wiring in the machine adapter), IEC, input, storage, tape.\n139: 6. Audio, device side effects, reset, snapshot, determinism, host pacing. `FR-VIC20-005` cartridge criteria (FE3, Ultimem, Mega-Cart) close here.\n140: 7. Restored-checkpoint lockstep: VSF Slice 4 (`Vic20ViceSnapshotLockstepTests.RestoredCheckpoint_MatchesXvicFor100000Cycles`) with the generated `ready-xvic-pal-unexpanded.vsf` and `ready-xvic-ntsc-unexpanded.vsf` fixtures from the ROM allowlist in the VSF plan.\n141: \n142: Exit: from-reset 100,000-cycle full-field lockstep green for `vic20` and `vic20ntsc` on the manifest workloads; restored-checkpoint lockstep green; `Vic20DeterminismTests` green; full suite green; HV AGREE; `PLAN-VICEORACLE-001` task 6 done.\n143: \n144: ### Phase G: Whole-goal closure\n145: \n146: Entry: Phases E and F accepted.\n147: \n148: Work: VSF Slice 5 (managed-to-native write so both machines round-trip through the native writer, `NativeViceSnapshotRoundTripTests.*` for all four directions); `tools/check_requirement_traceability.ps1` and `tools/check_phase_acceptance_coverage.ps1` run (today's known debt: 86 unreferenced canonical IDs and 1,011 noncanonical references, not a clean gate); `git diff --check`; wiki export through the plugin; requirement satisfaction set only through the plugin after the final HV; rewrite the root `HANDOFF.md` to the end state; commit and push only on a file-named order.\n149: \n150: Exit: the definition of done in section 1. VSF Slices 6 and 7, `PLAN-C64SET-001`, the dense-session proof, RomM, and UI plans remain their own TODOs (decision D1).\n151: \n152: ## 5. Risks and blockers\n153: \n154: - Observer feasibility (Phase C) is the single largest risk. Optimized and inlined paths (`vic20cpu.c` fast fetch, inline `alarm_set`) hide the bus from link-time wrapping; the two CDB breakpoint probes found nothing. Mitigation: stay in mechanism order, retain every failed probe, and stop at a named blocker rather than lower the field set. A blocker leaves the goal incomplete; it is not a completion condition.\n155: - Full-suite health. Zero failed and zero skipped is required at every exit, and the current tree carries 52 historical C64 acceptance failures (Sept 30), five CPU/NMI failures from the merge, three `Skip =` sites in the harness (measured today), and `PLAN-NATIVERESIDUE-003` instability. Phase A measures it; Phase E pays it down. Do not weaken assertions to pass.\n156: - The CPU engine rewrite (Phase E step 4) is the highest blast radius: `Mos6502.cs`, opcode files, `SystemClock` steal/BA, snapshot staging, `LockstepValidator`, C64 and VIC-20 lockstep, and every test that pins the hybrid phase flags. It needs its own decision-complete slice plan and hostile plan review before code.\n157: - Requirement drift. The R0 delta is accepted but unapplied; `FR-VIC-001` and `FR-VIC20-001` carry inconsistent status fields; the mapping helper can replacement-upsert and lose links (McpServer triage `5deae93eb0764736ae97184817d14eb6`). Apply one reviewed delta under a verified exclusive-writer window with full readback, never overlapping drafts.\n158: - Branch divergence. The Linux workspace published to `main` while this chat works on `develop`. A second merge of the same files is likely unless one branch is declared the only implementation branch.\n159: - Reviewer availability. Opus 5.5 max hit session limits twice (Oct 8 noon, Oct 11 R0 round). Review time is unbounded; do not schedule retries, and resume the same logical review where the CLI supports it.\n160: - Native restoration is destructive and irreversible for the untracked paths unless copied first. Phase D step 4 copies and hash-verifies before any removal.\n161: - Prior unresolved process items still ride along: the F-02 credential-exposure disposition, four historical reviewer-queue records, 26 pending plus 26 failsafe plus 2 quarantine plugin items, and the Oct 16 publication exclusions (465 files, 113 formerly tracked). None blocks lockstep work; none is closed by this handoff.\nFILE: docs/receipts/handoff/HANDOFF.lockstep-plan.v2.md (285 lines)\n62: ## 4. The plan\n63: \n64: Phases A to G keep v1's structure, entry and exit conditions. Amendments are marked. Each phase exit carries its own full-suite receipt, native inventory unchanged, request and response JSONL under `docs/receipts/hv/{yyyyMMddTHHmmss}-{gate}.*`, and AGREE 98/98.\n65: \n66: ### Phase A: Measured baselines on both machines, store reconciliation, decisions (no product code)\n67: \n68: Entry: this handoff; `develop` at `d00e8c5` or later on both machines; decisions D1 to D12 answered or explicitly deferred by Payton.\n69: \n70: Work (amended):\n71: 1. Windows baseline on PAYTON-LEGION2 exactly as v1 Phase A steps 1 to 3 (three native-safe suites unfiltered in Release; diagnostic TestHarness lockstep and acceptance filters against the current prebuilt dirty shim without invoking `Vice.Native.proj`; receipt under `docs/receipts/lockstep/{yyyyMMddTHHmmss}-phase-a-baseline-windows/`).\n72: 2. Linux baseline on LAB-OMARCHY: the same three native-safe suites; the same TestHarness filters against the Linux shim if `native/build-vice-shim.sh` output exists without rebuilding (if the harness build cannot avoid `Vice.Native.proj`, stop and report); `Category=Determinism`; the five `DIAG-CPUINT-001` failures re-run by name; receipt under `docs/receipts/lockstep/{yyyyMMddTHHmmss}-phase-a-baseline-linux/`. Pin SHA256 of `LockstepValidator.cs` and the six lockstep test files on both machines and record whether they match.\n73: 3. Store reconciliation per D10 to D12, executed on LEGION2 first and mirrored: resolve the 27 content conflicts, the 66 rejected ids, the 17 `closed` statuses. Then re-export both stores and prove equality by `Compare-McpRequirementExports.ps1` (zero onlyInSource, zero onlyInTarget, zero differing).\n74: 4. TODO alignment: mirror the lockstep TODO containers to LAB-OMARCHY or link the LAB-OMARCHY `DIAG-*` TODOs into `PLAN-VICEORACLE-001` and `PLAN-C64SET-001` on LEGION2 (D13). No other TODO or requirement mutation.\n75: 5. Decide D2 (R0 delta) before Phase E starts, because it rewrites `FR-CPU-004` and `TEST-CPU-001` criteria that Phase E must satisfy.\n76: \n77: Exit: both baseline receipts exist; both stores compare equal; decisions recorded in the session turn; no HV required (operational class).\n78: \n79: ### Phase B: Accept the native-safe mock gate (immutable Phase 1)\n80: \n81: Unchanged from v1. Runs on PAYTON-LEGION2 (the 826-input packet and remediation receipts live there). Exit adds: the three native-safe projects are also green unfiltered on LAB-OMARCHY (they are native-safe by construction), recorded in the same receipt.\n82: \n83: ### Phase C: External bus observer and cycle control for both machines (immutable Phase 2)\n84: \n85: Unchanged from v1, on the separate materialization under `artifacts/immutable-vice/` on PAYTON-LEGION2. Amendment: step 2 (stdio diagnostic adapter bound to a real C binding) is built and proved on both hosts, because the Linux shim build path (`build-vice-shim.sh`) must consume the same external translation units later in Phase D.\n86: \n87: ### Phase D: Native-safe builds and restoration of the oracle (immutable Phase 3)\n88: \n89: Unchanged from v1 with one amendment to step 1: `native/build-vice-shim.ps1` and `build-vice-shim-xvic.ps1` are the canonical scripts; the existing `build-vice-shim.sh` and `build-vice-shim-xvic.sh` are retired or made thin launchers of the PowerShell scripts (operator rule: PowerShell only), and the Linux materialization is created under `artifacts/immutable-vice/` on LAB-OMARCHY by the same script. Step 4 (restore `native/vice`) is executed on both machines, each after its own copy-and-hash preservation receipt, because the dirty states differ (12 modified / 2,527 untracked on LEGION2; 8 modified / 163 porcelain entries on LAB-OMARCHY).\n90: \n91: ### Phase E: C64 half-cycle lockstep against the immutable oracle\n92: \n93: Unchanged from v1 with these amendments:\n94: - Step 2 names the five failures now known (section 2.3) as the first slice. Each is reconciled to its VICE-derived AC (`FR-CPU-002` ac-05 phase sentence; `TR-CYCLE-001` AC1 to AC3 for interrupt pipeline stage) before any assertion is touched. `DIAG-CPUINT-001` closes only through this slice.\n95: - `TR-CYCLE-001` must exist on the store where the gate is claimed. If D11 keeps the current id, LAB-OMARCHY cannot hold it, and every Phase E gate is claimed on LEGION2.\n96: - Step 5 (restored-checkpoint lockstep) consumes the merged versioned managed snapshots from PR7 as implementation input only; `DIAG-SNAPSHOT-001` (restored-game continuity) is folded into the VSF Slice 2/3 gate rather than closed separately.\n97: \n98: ### Phase F: VIC-20 half-cycle lockstep (Grok plan Slice 3c, immutable Phase 4)\n99: \n100: Unchanged from v1. Amendment: `FR-VIC20-001` (pending with 6 of 6 satisfied), `TEST-VIC20-001` (5 of 5) and `TR-VIC20-PIXEL-001` (3 of 3) are re-proved on the immutable xvic in repair pair 4 before their satisfied flags are trusted; until then they are treated as unproven (D8 extended).\n101: \n102: ### Phase G: Whole-goal closure\n103: \n104: Unchanged from v1. Amendment: closure requires both requirement stores equal (Phase A step 3 re-run) and both machines' full applicable suites green, each with its stated denominator.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
