{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_47","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'docs/plans/PLAN-OBSERVER-001.md' -StartLine 1 -LineCount 180; & './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'build/Build.cs' -StartLine 1 -LineCount 160; & './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/ViceSharp.TestHarness.csproj'\"","aggregated_output":"FILE: docs/plans/PLAN-OBSERVER-001.md (46 lines)\n1: # PLAN-OBSERVER-001: Phase C step 3 under option B (runtime table interposition with the fast path withheld)\n2: \n3: Prepared 2026-10-19T20:45 on LAB-OMARCHY at `develop` `3ef73d6` while Payton's ruling on the Phase C step 3 blocker is open, so that a choice of B starts work at once. Scope of record: `HANDOFF.lockstep-plan.md` section 4 Phase C steps 3 to 5, as amended by `HANDOFF.lockstep-plan.v2.md` section 4 and the section 11 decisions (D3 no hostile validation until the scope is code complete, D4 approved, D6 develop only, D8 accuracy first). Evidence base: `docs/receipts/lockstep/20261019T201000-phase-c-step3-windows/` (LEGION2 nm probe), `20261019T203900-phase-c-step3-option-analysis-linux/` (source analysis, cross-checked by LEGION2), the step 1 contracts (`OBSERVATION-RECORD-SLICE.md`, `OBSERVATION-LINE-SLICE.md`) and the step 2 binding (`b2ddf745`). This plan is not approved until Payton rules B; it is a plan, not code.\n4: \n5: ## 1. Mechanism (decision-complete)\n6: \n7: 1. Build target: the separate materialization `artifacts/immutable-vice/cc67418c.../source` on each host (byte-identical on both, `docs/receipts/native-vice/20261019T202542-linux-materialization/`), stock objects linked with external translation units only; `native/vice` untouched; every generated file inventoried and outside both trees.\n8: 2. Fast path withheld: `ld --wrap=mem_mmu_translate`. `__wrap_mem_mmu_translate` calls `__real_mem_mmu_translate` and then sets `*limit = 0` (and `*base = NULL`), so `JUMP` (`maincpu.c` 83 to 89) and `maincpu_resync_limits` (519 to 524) leave `bank_limit` 0 and every `FETCH_OPCODE` and `FETCH_PARAM` takes the `LOAD` branch (`vic20cpu.c` 125 to 134; `6510core.c` 2081 to 2107, 537 to 541). Emulated semantics unchanged by design; proven, not assumed (section 3).\n9: 3. Access capture (corrected 20:48 after LEGION2's review; the first draft said \"point the pointers at wrapper-owned tables and wrap `mem_toggle_watchpoints`\", which fails because the pointers are reassigned by intra-object calls that `--wrap` never sees: `vic20mem.c` line 523 calls `mem_toggle_watchpoints` from inside `vic20mem.c`, and on x64sc the static helper `mem_update_tab_ptrs` (`c64memsc.c` 166 to 186) re-points all four pointers on every `mem_pla_config_changed` (226 to 243), that is on every banking change). Corrected mechanism: the wrapper patches the contents of VICE's own tables, not the pointers, so every reassignment still lands on patched tables. After `mem_initialize_memory` (and `cartridge_init_config` and the internal expansions, `c64memsc.c` 917 to 924) the wrapper reads the current row through `_mem_read_tab_ptr` and `_mem_write_tab_ptr`, saves each original entry, and writes a trampoline per page into the row in place; on x64sc the 32 rows of the static `mem_read_tab[NUM_CONFIGS][0x101]` and `mem_write_tab` (110 to 111) are reached from the current row pointer and `mem_get_current_bank_config()` (222, global) by the array layout, verified at init by a self-test (`mem_read_tab_set(config, page, f)` (751, global) followed by a read of the computed address must yield `f`, else fail closed); the watch tables (`mem_read_tab_watch`, `_mem_read_tab_watch`) are reached by calling the global `mem_toggle_watchpoints(1, NULL)`, patching the row the pointer then shows, and toggling back. Each trampoline records `kind` (read, write, dummy read, dummy write by which pointer it is reached through, decided by comparing the live pointers at entry), `address`, the data byte (the original read's return value, or the stored value) and `maincpu_clk`, then calls the saved original. Table contents rewritten by VICE after the patch are the central hazard, and the per-cycle rescan is the only coverage guarantee (LEGION2 review 20:49, accepted): the writers are called from inside their own objects too (`c64memsc.c` 800, 806 and 829 call `mem_set_write_hook`, 855 to 862 call `mem_read_tab_set`; `vic20mem.c` 396 to 462 call `vic20_mem_enable_ram_block` and `vic20_mem_disable_ram_block` from the RAM-block paths that also run at runtime when an expansion resource is toggled), and `c64memsc.c` 644 to 667 writes `mem_read_tab[i][j]` and `mem_write_tab[i][j]` directly for the 256K expansion segments, which no function wrap can reach. Wrapping those four writers is dropped (20:54, S0 receipt `20261019T205400-phase-c-step3-registration-audit-linux`): `c64memsc.c` 613 to 628, 656, 695 and 717 to 736 compare table entries by function identity inside `mem_initialize_memory` to decide which pages the 256K, plus256k and plus60k expansions take over, so a re-patch fired from a wrapped setter during initialization would make those comparisons fail silently. Instead, since on both machines every content rewrite happens inside `mem_initialize_memory` (S0 (c)), which is global with cross-object callers only (`machine.c` 266 and 304; `c64-memory-hacks.c` 235 and 267; on xvic the resource setters, snapshot reader and cartridge objects), `--wrap=mem_initialize_memory` re-patches every row after `__real_mem_initialize_memory` returns, with the tables holding VICE's original pointers for the whole of the real call. The guarantee: in the wrapped per-cycle hook, the wrapper rescans the rows the four live pointers resolve to (read, write, dummy read, dummy write; at most four rows of 0x101 entries) and re-patches, counting every entry found unpatched as a fail-closed `Missing` for that cycle, never a silent gap. A row rewritten while not current is covered by the same rescan because a row becomes current only through a pointer reassignment that precedes the next access by at least one hook, which is exactly what the ordering proof must establish for every path: banking (`mem_pla_config_changed`), the resource setters (`vic20mem.c` 396 to 462), the 256K paths (`c64memsc.c` 644 to 667), cartridge configuration and the watchpoint toggle; where a rewrite can precede an access inside the same cycle, that access is a fail-closed `Missing`, and if the proof shows it can happen on a workload the lockstep needs, that is a named blocker. The S0 audit lists every assignment site of the four pointers and every writer of the table contents per machine (direct array writes included), classified as cross-object (wrappable) or intra-object (rescan only), with the thread on which each runs.\n10: 4. Half-cycle and phase: the per-cycle hook (`__wrap_vic_cycle` for xvic; the `vicii_cycle` equivalent for x64sc, named by the nm probe) advances the half-cycle index and emits the `VICE_OBS` line of the step 1 contract once per CPU cycle with the accesses captured since the previous hook, PHI1 for the dummy-read-only or idle half and PHI2 for the access half as VICE's single-cycle model exposes them; where VICE performs no access in a cycle the kind is `idle` and `data` is what the contract requires on idle half-cycles (the step 1 self-assessment's open item, resolved by evidence in S3: either the last bus value VICE exposes or a fail-closed `Missing:Data`, never an invented value).\n11: 5. Interrupt mask and stolen cycles: `maincpu_int_status->global_pending_int` (reachable global per the nm probe) for `interruptMask`; `stolen` from `maincpu_ba_low_flags` and `vicii_steal_cycles` on x64sc; on the VIC-20 `ba` and `stolen` follow Payton's Ba/Aec applicability ruling (constant 0 only if he rules \"not applicable\", with the contract amended to say so; otherwise `Missing:Ba` fail closed).\n12: 6. Output: `VICE_OBS` lines on stdout in binary mode on Windows (CRLF is a framing fault), through the retained stdio adapter path only after its retention contract is fixed (F-04), otherwise through a dedicated file descriptor opened by the wrapper so native stdout is not involved; decided in S2 by the F-04 state.\n13: 7. Pause and resume: the existing clock gate (`tools/immutable-vice/clock-gate.c`) stays the permission mechanism; the observation line precedes the gate's `VICE_GATE` record for the same cycle, so the host reads the record before permitting the next cycle (deterministic per-cycle pause and resume replacing `-limitcycles`).\n14: \n15: ## 2. Slices and gates (BDPv4, mocks first, no hostile validation until step 12)\n16: \n17: - S0 Registration and assignment audit (both hosts, read only; executed on LAB-OMARCHY 20:47 to 20:54, receipt `docs/receipts/lockstep/20261019T205400-phase-c-step3-registration-audit-linux/`, (a) confirmed independently by LEGION2 at 20:51; no blocker for B found): (a) enumerate every caller of the page registration that sets a non-null `read_base` (`vic20mem.c` 314 to 320: on xvic only the Kernal ROM at `0xe0` to `0xff` passes a non-null base, line 502 to 504, every other `set_mem` call at 336 to 499 passes `NULL`; x64sc: `mem_read_base_tab` defaults to `mem_ram` for RAM pages at `c64memsc.c` 805 to 831, chargen ROM at 882 to 898, BASIC and Kernal ROM in `c64meminit.c` 148 to 161 and 232 to 269, with `NULL` for the I/O and cartridge configurations at 202, 209, 279, 313, 343, 355, 361, 369 and 374; limits from `c64memlimit.c` 96 to 113) and classify each mapped page as side-effect-free or not; (b) enumerate every assignment site of the four table pointers and every writer of the table contents per machine, classified cross-object or intra-object; receipt with file and line per site. A side-effecting page on the fast path or an uncovered writer is a named blocker for B.\n18: - S1 Wrapper contract, mock first (NativeClockProbe.Tests; executed on LAB-OMARCHY 2026-10-20T07:33 to 07:43, receipt `docs/receipts/lockstep/20261020T074300-phase-c-step3-s1-bus-observer-linux/`: mock gate 12 passed, implementation 10 passed against the compiled wrapper, full project 175 passed, 0 skipped; S1b executed 07:46 to 07:52, receipt `20261020T075200-phase-c-step3-s1b-rows-linux/`: row mocks 15 passed, `--filter BusObserver` 50 passed, full project 203 passed, 0 skipped; LEGION2 ran the S1 gates on Windows with equal counts; S1c executed 07:53 to 07:57, receipt `20261020T075700-phase-c-step3-s1c-watch-rows-linux/`: watch rows learned from the pointers and from the wrapped toggle, full project 211 passed, 0 skipped; open: the Windows run of S1b and S1c): trampoline capture record and ordering fixtures (read, write, dummy, address, data, clock), the `limit 0` translate rule, the rescan rule (unpatched entry becomes a fail-closed `Missing`), the initialization-timing rule (no patch while the real `mem_initialize_memory` runs, re-patch of every row after it returns), with negative controls (wrong kind, dropped access, wrong clock, restored stock table, patch during initialization); then the compiled C fixture binding through `CompiledCFixture` on both hosts.\n19: - S2a Adapter (executed on LAB-OMARCHY 2026-10-20T08:00 to 08:03, receipt `20261020T080200-phase-c-step3-s2a-bus-adapter-linux/`, full project 219 passed, 0 skipped): `bus-adapter.c` wraps `vice_sharp_observe_clock`, runs the observer hook then the unmodified gate adapter, sinks `VICE_BUS,<kind>,<detail>` on stderr ahead of `VICE_GATE`, stops with 72 on a sink failure; item 6 decided as stderr pending operator-confirm item 3.\n20: - S2b Build (executed on LAB-OMARCHY 2026-10-20T08:09 to 08:19, receipt `20261020T081800-phase-c-step3-s2b-observer-build-linux/`: `x64sc-observer` 72FDA032..., `xvic-observer` B8186DDF..., all wrap symbols present, materialization unchanged; Windows run pending Payton's clearance and must run inside MSYS2 per the 08:07 directive): PowerShell build script per host (`native/build-vice-observer.ps1`, canonical; Linux gcc, Windows MSYS2 gcc by full path) linking the stock objects with `clock-observer.c`, `clock-gate.c`, `gate-observer.c`, `bus-observer.c`, `bus-adapter.c` and `--wrap=vic_cycle,--wrap=mem_mmu_translate,--wrap=mem_initialize_memory,--wrap=mem_toggle_watchpoints,--wrap=vice_sharp_observe_clock` (x64sc adds its cycle hook and `-DVICE_SHARP_ROWS=32`); link launch and result JSON, object hashes, inventory before and after.\n21: - S3 proof items from source (LAB-OMARCHY 2026-10-20T08:20 to 08:24, Linux headless build): (a) `mem_initialize_memory` performs no bus access through the rows while it runs: `vic20mem.c` 369 to 526 contains only `set_mem` registrations, direct row assignments, the two pointer assignments, the intra-object toggle and `maincpu_resync_limits` (which calls the wrapped translate, not a row); `c64memsc.c` 774 to 929 likewise (no `_mem_read_tab_ptr[`, `LOAD` or `STORE` in either range; the cartridge configuration bodies it calls were not read and are left to the trace evidence). (b) Single emulation thread: the Linux headless build has `USE_VICE_THREAD` undefined (`build-linux/src/config.h` 783), so `main.c` 489 runs `main_loop_forever` on the process's only thread; the only `pthread_create` in the non-arch sources is the `USE_VICE_THREAD` path at `main.c` 478. The Windows MSYS2 build's `config.h` is LEGION2's to cite. (c) Everything outside `mem_initialize_memory` is covered by the per-cycle rescan (S0, LEGION2 enclosure check).\n22: - S3 Equivalence runs (executed on LAB-OMARCHY 2026-10-20T08:24 to 09:06, receipt `20261020T084500-phase-c-step3-s3-equivalence-linux/`): for xvic and x64sc at 100,000 and 1,000,000 cycles, the stock, withheld and keep runs of the ROM-only reset workload have byte-identical exit screenshots, identical `VICE_GATE` sequences, and every access the keep run observes occurs in the withheld run with the same kind, address, data and clock, once the VICE RAM-init random bits are pinned with `-seed 1` (without the seed, dummy stack reads of uninitialized RAM differ in the data byte between any two processes; a workload parameter, not a wrapper defect). One wrapper defect found and fixed mocks first on the way: a store that itself reconfigures the banks (`STX $01`) was reported with kind missing; the kind is now decided at entry and the dummy pointers are healed after every access. Register traces are not available per cycle from outside the CPU core (registers are locals of the interpreter loop), so the per-cycle claim rests on the bus trace, which is what the lockstep consumes.\n23: - S3 Equivalence proof as planned: ROM-only workloads from `docs/requirements/oracles/vic20-xvic-manifest.json` and the C64 reset workload run twice, with and without the wrapped translate, with the gate permitting every cycle: register, clock and `VICE_GATE` traces byte-identical, total cycles equal, and the S0 classification cited; any difference is a named blocker.\n24: - S4 plan (written 2026-10-20T09:13 after D15 and confirm item 3; decision-complete, mocks first per sub-slice):\n25:   - S4a x64sc stolen cycles. `vicii_steal_cycles` (`viciisc/vicii-cycle.c` 628 to 636) loops `maincpu_clk++; ba_low = vicii_cycle();` inside its own object, so the per-cycle hook and the gate never see stolen cycles (the 1M reset workload had no `$D011` write and therefore no badline, which is why the gate sequence showed no gap). The wrapper adds `--wrap=vicii_steal_cycles` (called cross-object from `mainc64cpu.c` 118 compiled into `c64cpusc.o`) and executes the same three statements itself, calling `vicii_cycle` cross-object so every stolen cycle reaches the hook with a `stolen` flag set; the real function is not called. This is the one place the wrapper restates VICE statements rather than forwarding them; proof: the S3 runner extended with a workload long enough to enable the display (4,000,000 cycles) must show no gap in the clock sequence, identical screenshots and per-cycle traces, and the stolen-cycle count equal between withheld and keep.\n26:   - S4b `VICE_OBS` composition in a new `obs-adapter.c` (events on stderr ahead of `VICE_GATE`, confirm item 3): per CPU cycle two records, PHI1 then PHI2, half-cycle index `2n` and `2n+1` for hook index `n` since process start (the observed reset is the first thing the trace shows). PHI2 carries the cycle's CPU access (`read`, `write`, `dummy` for either dummy kind) with its address and data; a cycle without a CPU access (stolen, or none observed) is `idle`. PHI1 is `idle`. Field sources, x64sc: `ba` from the flags `vicii_cycle` returned for the cycle (`vice_sharp_vicii_ba_flags`, 1 when BA high); `stolen` from S4a; `interruptMask` from `maincpu_int_status->global_pending_int` (`interrupt.h` 55 to 128, read through the real header in the production build with a static assertion on the field offset, and through a mirrored fixture header in the tests); PHI1 `data` from `vicii_read_phi1()` (`viciisc/vicii-phi1.c` 34, the last PHI1 bus value); `aec` derived from BA per the VIC-II rule that the VIC takes the bus three cycles after BA goes low (Bauer, \"The MOS 6567/6569 video controller\", section 3.14.3), which VICE SC embeds in the three-cycle write window of `mainc64cpu.c` 117 to 154; this derivation and the PHI1 `address` (the CPU is off the bus; reported as 0 under a per-field applicability note) are the two contract amendments S4b writes into `OBSERVATION-RECORD-SLICE.md` for Payton to confirm or reject. xvic (D15): `ba` 0, `aec` 1, `stolen` 0 constant under the applicability table; `interruptMask` as above; PHI1 `data` from `vic.vbuf` (`vic20/victypes.h` 207, `vic-cycle.c` 300) through the real header.\n27:   - S4c Real traces: the S3 workloads plus the 4M display-on workload,