n32: \n33: ## 3. Risks and named blockers\n34: \n35: - A side-effecting read on a `read_base` page (S0) or a trace difference in S3: blocker, reported, no workaround by inference.\n36: - Performance: every access through a trampoline slows VICE; acceptable for gated lockstep runs, measured in S4.\n37: - The dummy tables' coverage of every dummy access depends on VICE routing all dummy reads through `_mem_read_tab_ptr_dummy`; S1 fixtures include `FETCH_PARAM_DUMMY` with the fast path withheld (`6510core.c` 538 to 541) and the `STORE_DUMMY` path, cited by line in the receipt.\n38: - F-04 (stdout retention) decides the output channel (section 1, item 6).\n39: \n40: ## 4. Operator-confirm items\n41: \n42: 1. Rule B (this plan), C, or D for step 3.\n43: 2. Rule Ba/Aec applicability for the VIC-20.\n44: 3. Approve the output-channel choice in section 1 item 6 once F-04's state is known, or choose the dedicated descriptor now.\n45: \n46: Signed: claude-fable-5-1-high@ClaudeCode | Confidence: correctness 93%, accuracy 97%, completeness 90%\nFILE: build/Build.cs (2033 lines)\n1: using System;\n2: using System.Collections.Generic;\n3: using System.IO;\n4: using System.IO.Compression;\n5: using System.Linq;\n6: using System.Text.Json;\n7: using System.Xml.Linq;\n8: using Nuke.Common;\n9: using Nuke.Common.CI.AzurePipelines;\n10: using Nuke.Common.IO;\n11: using Nuke.Common.ProjectModel;\n12: using Nuke.Common.Tooling;\n13: using Nuke.Common.Tools.DotNet;\n14: using Nuke.Common.Tools.Git;\n15: using static Nuke.Common.Tools.DotNet.DotNetTasks;\n16: using static Nuke.Common.Tools.Git.GitTasks;\n17: \n18: // Azure DevOps pipelines (hand-maintained YAML: AutoGenerate = false).\n19: // Pipeline definitions in ADO are wired to GitHub sharpninja/vice-sharp (not\n20: // Azure Repos) so v* tags and main pushes on origin fire CI/release.\n21: // Both dispatch to the self-hosted \"Default\" agent pool via\n22: // DefaultPoolAzurePipelinesAttribute (native toolchain + NUGET_API_KEY).\n23: