{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths @('docs/requirements/functional/FR-Vice-Reference.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Vice-Oracle.md','docs/requirements/test/TEST-Vice-Oracle.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Cycle-Accuracy.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Native-Vice-Snapshot.md','docs/requirements/traceability/VICE-Immutable-Oracle-Matrix-2026-10-03.md'); & './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'native/build-vice-observer.ps1' -StartLine 231 -LineCount 90\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_53","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command \"& './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths @('docs/requirements/functional/FR-Vice-Reference.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Vice-Oracle.md','docs/requirements/test/TEST-Vice-Oracle.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Cycle-Accuracy.md','docs/requirements/technical/TR-Native-Vice-Snapshot.md','docs/requirements/traceability/VICE-Immutable-Oracle-Matrix-2026-10-03.md'); & './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'native/build-vice-observer.ps1' -StartLine 231 -LineCount 90\"","aggregated_output":"FILE: docs/requirements/functional/FR-Vice-Reference.md (26 lines)\n1: # FR-Vice-Reference: Explicit Integrity Guardrail for the Native VICE Behavioral Target\n2: \n3: ## FR-VICE-001: Preserve the established immutable native VICE behavioral target\n4: \n5: **ID:** FR-VICE-001  \n6: **Priority:** Critical  \n7: **Status:** Pending  \n8: **Area:** VICE\n9: \n10: ### Requirement\n11: \n12: This requirement does not introduce new emulator behavior. It makes the original VICE-as-reference contract executable: ViceSharp shall conform managed emulator behavior to a pinned, unmodified native VICE revision. Native VICE source and oracle behavior shall never be changed to make ViceSharp pass. Observation and control integrations must be implemented as wrappers outside `native/vice`.\n13: \n14: The underlying authority predates this guardrail. The first committed `TR-CYCLE-001` names VICE x64sc as the reference implementation and accepted gold standard, while `FR-SNP-005` and `TR-LOCKSTEP-VSF-001` preserve native x64sc and xvic as the behavioral and file-compatibility oracles.\n15: \n16: ### Acceptance Criteria\n17: \n18: 1. **AC1:** Every parity or lockstep acceptance run identifies the pinned `native/vice` commit and proves no tracked `native/vice` file differs from that commit before or after the run.\n19: 2. **AC2:** Emulator-behavior corrections are made in managed ViceSharp. Native integration code may wrap or observe VICE only from outside `native/vice` and may not alter target source or behavior.\n20: 3. **AC3:** Oracle binaries used as acceptance evidence are built from the pinned unmodified VICE target. Evidence from a patched or behavior-modified VICE build is rejected.\n21: 4. **AC4:** When an external wrapper cannot obtain a required observation from immutable VICE, the parity gate fails closed and reports the missing observation instead of inferring data or changing VICE.\n22: \n23: ### Traceability\n24: \n25: - Technical requirement: `TR-VICE-ORACLE-001`\n26: - Test requirement: `TEST-VICE-001`\nFILE: docs/requirements/technical/TR-Vice-Oracle.md (29 lines)\n1: # TR-Vice-Oracle: Read-Only Native VICE Oracle Boundary\n2: \n3: ## TR-VICE-ORACLE-001: Read-only native VICE oracle boundary\n4: \n5: **ID:** TR-VICE-ORACLE-001  \n6: **Priority:** Critical  \n7: **Status:** Pending  \n8: **Area:** VICE  \n9: **Subarea:** ORACLE\n10: \n11: ### Requirement\n12: \n13: The `native/vice` gitlink is a read-only behavioral oracle. All stepping, tracing, snapshot, bus-event, and lifecycle integration must be implemented by external wrappers, launchers, link-time interposition, or public VICE interfaces that leave the pinned target source and semantics unchanged.\n14: \n15: No patch application, generated source injection, or direct source edit under `native/vice` is permitted.\n16: \n17: ### Acceptance Criteria\n18: \n19: 1. **AC1:** The native oracle preflight fails before validation when any tracked file under `native/vice` differs from the pinned gitlink commit.\n20: 2. **AC2:** Wrapper builds and tests do not edit, generate source into, or apply patches to tracked `native/vice` paths.\n21: 3. **AC3:** Required CPU bus events, timing, snapshots, and lifecycle observations come from real immutable VICE execution through external wrapper mechanisms. `PeekBus`, PC-derived values, fabricated events, and modified native builds are forbidden substitutes.\n22: 4. **AC4:** Each oracle receipt records the `native/vice` commit, wrapper version or hash, clean-target proof, exact command, and zero-failure/zero-skip result for the claimed scope.\n23: 5. **AC5:** A missing wrapper capability is an explicit blocker and cannot be remediated by modifying `native/vice`.\n24: \n25: ### Traceability\n26: \n27: - Functional requirement: `FR-VICE-001`\n28: - Test requirement: `TEST-VICE-001`\n29: \nFILE: docs/requirements/test/TEST-Vice-Oracle.md (25 lines)\n1: # TEST-Vice-Oracle: Immutable Native VICE Oracle Integrity Gates\n2: \n3: ## TEST-VICE-001: Immutable native VICE oracle integrity gates\n4: \n5: **ID:** TEST-VICE-001  \n6: **Priority:** Critical  \n7: **Status:** Pending  \n8: **Area:** VICE\n9: \n10: ### Condition\n11: \n12: Verify that every native parity oracle uses the pinned unmodified VICE target and that wrapper-only integration fails closed when required target observations are unavailable.\n13: \n14: ### Acceptance Criteria\n15: \n16: 1. **AC1:** A clean-target preflight passes for the pinned `native/vice` commit, and a simulated dirty-target fixture is rejected before parity execution.\n17: 2. **AC2:** The wrapper integration test proves no tracked `native/vice` file changes across build and execution.\n18: 3. **AC3:** Oracle receipts prove native commit identity, clean tracked source, wrapper provenance, exact commands, and failed 0 skipped 0 for the claimed gate.\n19: 4. **AC4:** When a required real bus or timing observation is unavailable, the test fails at that first missing observation and does not substitute `PeekBus`, PC inference, fabricated data, or a modified native build.\n20: \n21: ### Traceability\n22: \n23: - Functional requirement: `FR-VICE-001`\n24: - Technical requirement: `TR-VICE-ORACLE-001`\n25: \nFILE: docs/requirements/technical/TR-Cycle-Accuracy.md (65 lines)\n1: # TR-Cycle-Accuracy: Cycle Accuracy Technical Requirement\n2: \n3: ## Document Information\n4: \n5: | Field          | Value                          |\n6: |----------------|--------------------------------|\n7: | Quality Area   | Accuracy / Fidelity            |\n8: | Version        | 0.1.0-draft                    |\n9: | Last Updated   | 2026-10-07                     |\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: ## TR-CYCLE-001: Sub-Cycle Bus-Phase Accuracy\n14: \n15: **ID:** TR-CYCLE-001\n16: **Title:** Sub-Cycle Bus-Phase Accuracy Matching VICE x64sc Behavior\n17: **Priority:** P0 -- Critical\n18: **Category:** Accuracy\n19: **Status:** Pending; all seven structured acceptance criteria remain unsatisfied.\n20: \n21: The supported MCP update/readback on 2026-10-07 corrected an unsupported `closed` status without changing the requirement description or any acceptance criterion. Receipt: `docs/receipts/hv/20261007T131928-0500-phase0-mock-scope-grok.cycle-status-remediation.json`. A bus-address comparator fixture alone does not cover criterion 4; PHI2/AEC gating requires its own evidence.\n22: \n23: ### Description\n24: \n25: The emulation core shall operate at sub-cycle granularity, modeling the two phases of the system clock (PHI1 and PHI2) and the bus ownership arbitration between the CPU and VIC-II. This level of accuracy is required to correctly emulate cycle-exact tricks used by C64 demos and games. The reference implementation for correctness is VICE x64sc (the cycle-exact VICE variant).\n26: \n27: ### Rationale\n28: \n29: Many C64 programs rely on exact cycle timing for visual effects (raster bars, FLI, sprite multiplexing, open borders) and audio effects (digi playback). A cycle-accurate emulator that does not model bus phases correctly will fail these programs. VICE x64sc is the accepted gold standard for cycle-accurate C64 emulation.\n30: \n31: ### Technical Specification\n32: \n33: 1. **Clock Model:** The system clock is divided into PHI1 (VIC-II access phase) and PHI2 (CPU access phase). Each phase is one half-cycle.\n34: 2. **Bus Arbitration:** During PHI1, the VIC-II has bus access (for c-access, g-access, p-access, s-access). During PHI2, the CPU has bus access (unless the VIC-II has asserted BA to steal cycles).\n35: 3. **BA/AEC Signals:** The VIC-II asserts BA (Bus Available = low) 3 cycles before it needs to steal PHI2 cycles, giving the CPU time to complete its current access. AEC (Address Enable Control) gates the CPU's address bus.\n36: 4. **CPU Pipeline:** The CPU's internal pipeline (fetch, decode, execute sub-phases) is modeled per-cycle, not per-instruction.\n37: 5. **Tick Granularity:** The `IClockedDevice.Tick()` method is invoked once per half-cycle (PHI1 or PHI2), not once per full cycle.\n38: \n39: ### Acceptance Criteria\n40: \n41: 1. All devices (CPU, VIC-II, CIA, SID) are ticked at half-cycle (bus phase) granularity.\n42: 2. The VIC-II's DMA steal pattern (badlines, sprites) matches VICE x64sc cycle-by-cycle.\n43: 3. CIA timer countdown occurs at the correct bus phase (PHI2 falling edge).\n44: 4. CPU memory accesses occur only during PHI2 when AEC is asserted.\n45: 5. The Lorenz test suite passes at 100% (same pass rate as VICE x64sc).\n46: 6. The VICE cycle-exact test programs (e.g., those in the VICE test suite repository) produce identical output.\n47: 7. Raster effects that depend on sub-cycle timing (FLI, AGSP, sprite stretching) work correctly.\n48: \n49: ### Verification Method\n50: \n51: - Automated test suite comparing ViceSharp output to VICE x64sc reference captures.\n52: - Lorenz test suite full execution with pass/fail comparison.\n53: - Visual comparison of known demo effects (Crest, Booze Design, Oxyron productions).\n54: \n55: ### Related FRs\n56: \n57: - FR-CPU-002 (Cycle-accurate execution timing)\n58: - FR-VIC-006 (Badline handling and DMA stealing)\n59: - FR-VIC-010 (Sprite multiplexing DMA timing)\n60: \n61: ### Design Decisions\n62: \n63: - Half-cycle ticking doubles the tick rate compared to full-cycle emulators but is essential for bus-phase accuracy.\n64: - The clock driver is a single-threaded loop that alternates PHI1/PHI2 ticks to all devices in deterministic order.\n65: - The tick order within a phase is: VIC-II, then CIA1, then CIA2, then SID, then CPU (matching the hardware's analog timing).\nFILE: docs/requirements/technical/TR-Native-Vice-Snapshot.md (83 lines)\n1: # TR-Native-Vice-Snapshot: Native VICE VSF Snapshot Technical Requirement\n2: \n3: ## Document Information\n4: \n5: | Field          | Value                         |\n6: |----------------|-------------------------------|\n7: | Quality Area   | Compatibility / Determinism   |\n8: | Version        | 0.1.0-draft                   |\n9: | Last Updated   | 2026-10-04                    |\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: ## TR-LOCKSTEP-VSF-001: Native VICE VSF Snapshot Lockstep\n14: \n15: **ID:** TR-LOCKSTEP-VSF-001\n16: **Title:** Native VICE VSF Container and Deterministic Resume Compatibility\n17: **Priority:** P0 -- Critical\n18: **Category:** Compatibility / Correctness\n19: **Status:** Pending\n20: \n21: ### Description\n22: \n23: ViceSharp shall productionize and complete its existing managed VSF resume bridge so native VICE `.vsf` is the canonical snapshot implementation for the supported x64sc C64 and xvic VIC-20 product paths. The existing path asks native VICE to decode its version-specific modules, exports the resumed state through `IViceNative`, and injects that state into the managed machine. The production bridge shall also transfer managed state into the matching native instance so VICE itself writes the output `.vsf`.\n24: \n25: ### Acceptance Criteria\n26: \n27: 1. Native `.vsf` load and save use the matching pinned VICE snapshot implementation through the existing `IViceNative.ReadSnapshot` and `WriteSnapshot` bridge. The managed envelope walk accepts the current 58-byte and pinned-reader-compatible legacy 37-byte file headers, reads each 22-byte module header with a little-endian inclusive total length, reaches exact EOF, and preserves VICE machine identity, container/module versions, byte order, framing, and restore-order behavior without parsing version-specific payloads.\n28: 2. The existing managed C64 staging path is moved from the test harness into production and completed, and an equivalent VIC-20 adapter is added. Reusable CPU, video, CIA, VIA, SID, memory, cartridge, tape, and drive state contracts remain machine-agnostic while machine wiring stays in the owning machine or device adapter.\n29: 3. Snapshot application uses the same non-blocking try-create ABI in both native DLLs, treats C64 and xvic as independent per-DLL singleton leases, bounds acquisition to ten seconds with cancellation, finishes envelope validation plus native read and complete immutable state export before managed mutation, revalidates session generation/profile/media under the session lock, and restores a complete rollback bundle on apply failure. Rollback failure stops and faults the session until it is recreated.\n30: 4. Snapshot state preserves every cycle-visible CPU architectural and in-flight pipeline, bus, banking, raster/video pipeline, interrupt, timer, serial, oscillator/envelope/filter, peripheral, media, drive, and cartridge value required for deterministic continuation. Resetting in-flight CPU state is not accepted as full import.\n31: 5. Native x64sc and xvic remain the behavioral and file-compatibility implementations and oracles. Every normal supported snapshot API and Avalonia `win-x64` file, quick-slot, and ten-named-slot command invokes the native `.vsf` bridge by default; hosts without that packaged capability return unavailable without a proprietary fallback. UWP/Xbox implementation is out of scope and its historical requirements are not retired.\n32: 6. The existing `vice-sharp.machine-snapshot.v2` and `vice-sharp.runtime-snapshot.v1` readers are isolated behind the explicit protobuf `ImportLegacySnapshot(RestoreSnapshotRequest) returns (RestoreSnapshotResponse)` operation and matching `ISnapshotService.ImportLegacySnapshotAsync`. Normal `RestoreSnapshotAsync` rejects those formats with a migration error, no normal capture or save emits them, and replay remains a separate service. Manually injected CPU-plus-RAM state or native-only shim success does not satisfy this requirement.\n33: 7. The supported `win-x64` desktop publish and MSI build both shims, recursively verify their PE imports, stage only the proven loose non-system closure after `DotNetPublish` and before WiX harvest, include VICE license/source-offer material, contain no ROM images, and pass an installed default C64 and VIC-20 native-VSF capture/restore smoke test. Unsupported RIDs report native VSF unavailable instead of silently omitting the capability.\n34: \n35: ### Architecture Constraints\n36: \n37: - Use `vice.native.vsf` as the canonical public `SnapshotDto.Format` identifier for supported C64 and VIC-20 snapshots.\n38: - Inventory the exact mandatory and optional module names, versions, represented state, and restore order from the pinned VICE source in `docs/requirements/oracles/vice-vsf-state-transfer-manifest.json` before the existing bridge is promoted. This manifest drives state-transfer coverage, not a replacement VSF parser.\n39: - Keep native VSF parsing and writing in native VICE. Put state transfer and wiring in production C64 and VIC-20 adapters built around machine-agnostic state records.\n40: - Load and validate the VSF in a short-lived native instance, export it into immutable staged state, and validate all required state before the live session lock is entered.\n41: - Capture a complete pre-apply rollback state, apply under `EmulatorRuntimeSession.SyncRoot`, and restore the rollback state before releasing the lock if any adapter rejects the staged state.\n42: - Normal snapshot capture and save operations never emit a proprietary format. Retain the two existing proprietary readers only through `ImportLegacySnapshotAsync`; it restores legacy state into the current session once, and the next normal save produces native `.vsf`.\n43: \n44: ### Source References\n45: \n46: - `native/vice/vice/src/snapshot.c`\n47: - `native/vice/vice/src/snapshot.h`\n48: - `native/vice/vice/src/c64/c64-snapshot.c`\n49: - `native/vice/vice/src/vic20/vic20-snapshot.c`\n50: - Pinned VICE commit: `cc67418cba58e71d8e70fd353e9de47199ad2075`\n51: \n52: ### Traceability\n53: \n54: - **Functional Requirement:** FR-SNP-005\n55: - **Test Requirement:** TEST-SNP-002\n56: - **Related Requirements:** FR-SNP-001, FR-SNP-002, TR-DET-001\n57: - **Recovered Legacy Requirements:** FR-VSFLOCKSTEP-001, TR-VSFLOCKSTEP-RESUME-001, TEST-VSFLOCKSTEP-001\n58: - **Plan:** `docs/plans/PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001.md`\n59: - **Gate 0 local evidence:** `docs/receipts/hv/20261003T100623-0500-vsf-gate0-local-evidence.md`\n60: \n61: ---\n62: \n63: ## TR-VSFLOCKSTEP-RESUME-001: Backward-Compatible Snapshot Resume\n64: \n65: **ID:** TR-VSFLOCKSTEP-RESUME-001\n66: **Title:** Backward-compatible snapshot module read and VIC-II model alignment\n67: **Priority:** P1 -- High\n68: **Status:** Pending\n69: **Scope Layer:** `layer-1`\n70: \n71: ### Description\n72: \n73: An external compatibility adapter outside `native/vice` shall normalize legacy x64sc `MAINCPU` v1.2 and v1.3 modules for the pinned, unmodified VICE reader while current v1.4 modules remain byte-identical. The wrapper shall align the VIC-II model before `machine_read_snapshot` and normalize wrapper-reported `snapshot_last_error` after a successful read. No part of this compatibility path may edit, patch, generate into, or behaviorally change `native/vice`.\n74: \n75: ### Acceptance Criteria\n76: \n77: 1. An external compatibility adapter outside `native/vice` loads a validated `MAINCPU` v1.2 module by inserting the absent v1.3 `ane_log_level` and `lxa_log_level` fields and absent v1.4 `maincpu_jammed` field with the pinned immutable VICE defaults before `maincpu_ba_low_flags`, updates the inclusive module length, preserves every following byte and module, handles v1.3 by inserting only `maincpu_jammed`, and passes current v1.4 through byte-for-byte. Malformed or unsupported layouts fail closed. External v1.2 and v1.3 fixtures plus a current v1.4 native round-trip prove resume behavior with zero failed, zero skipped, and zero aborted prerequisites.\n78: 2. native/build-vice-shim.ps1 materializes the pinned VICE commit into a separate initially clean source root outside native/vice, follows the pinned VICE Team build instructions there (including required in-source build generation, explicitly approved 2026-10-08), and links only external wrapper translation units stored outside the submodule. Pre-build and post-build commit, tracked status and a complete inventory of original upstream source bytes must match exactly. Record all generated/build files separately with paths, hashes and producing commands; those artifacts are permitted only in the separate materialization/output roots and may not replace or modify original upstream source files. Any patch application, generated file or source mutation under native/vice fails the build. native/patches/vice-shim-runtime.patch is diagnostic legacy evidence only and is never applied.\n79: \n80: ### Traceability\n81: \n82: - **Functional Requirements:** FR-VSFLOCKSTEP-001, FR-SNP-005\n83: - **Test Requirements:** TEST-VSFLOCKSTEP-001, TEST-SNP-002\nFILE: docs/requirements/traceability/VICE-Immutable-Oracle-Matrix-2026-10-03.md (12 lines)\n1: # Immutable Native VICE Oracle Traceability\n2: \n3: Recorded from the operator directive on 2026-10-03: native VICE is the immutable behavioral target. Wrappers outside `native/vice` are allowed; changing native VICE is forbidden.\n4: \n5: - `FR-VICE-001` AC1-AC4 map to `TR-VICE-ORACLE-001` AC1-AC5.\n6: - `TEST-VICE-001` AC1 proves clean-target preflight and dirty-target rejection.\n7: - `TEST-VICE-001` AC2 proves wrapper build and execution leave tracked target source unchanged.\n8: - `TEST-VICE-001` AC3 proves target and wrapper provenance plus zero-failure/zero-skip evidence.\n9: - `TEST-VICE-001` AC4 proves missing observability fails closed without target modification or inferred events.\n10: \n11: No acceptance criterion is satisfied yet. The existing dirty `native/vice` checkout and binaries built from it are not acceptable completion evidence for these requirements.\n12: \nFILE: native/build-vice-observer.ps1 (320 lines)\n231:     foreach ($t in $targets) { if (-not $linkLines.ContainsKey($t)) { throw \"No link line captured for $t; see $log\" } }\n232:     Write-Output (\"make ok; link lines captured for {0}\" -f ($linkLines.Keys -join ', '))\n233: }\n234: \n235: function Step-Link {\n236:     $linkPath = Join-Path $receipts 'stock-link-lines.json'\n237:     if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $linkPath)) { throw \"Run the Make stage first: $linkPath missing\" }\n238:     $linkLines = Get-Content -LiteralPath $linkPath -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json\n239:     $results = @()\n240:     foreach ($t in Get-TargetList) {\n241:         $cycleHook = if ($t -eq 'x64sc') { 'clock-observer-x64sc.c' } else { 'clock-observer.c' }\n242:         $cycleSymbol = if ($t -eq 'x64sc') { 'vicii_cycle' } else { 'vic_cycle' }\n243:         $defines = if ($t -eq 'x64sc') { '-DVICE_SHARP_ROWS=32' } else { '' }\n244:         $objDir = Join-Path $build \"observer-obj/$t\"\n245:         New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $objDir | Out-Null\n246:         $objects = @()\n247:         # The machine glue (obs-fields-*.c) reads VICE globals through the real headers of the work tree and\n248:         # the configured config.h of the build tree; every other unit is header-free.\n249:         $includes = @((Join-Path $work 'src'), (Join-Path $build 'src'), (Join-Path $work 'src/vic20'), (Join-Path $work 'src/raster'), (Join-Path $work 'src/viciisc')) |\n250:             ForEach-Object { '-I\"' + (ConvertTo-ShellPath $_) + '\"' }\n251:         $units = @($cycleHook, 'clock-gate.c', 'gate-observer.c', 'bus-observer.c', 'bus-adapter.c', 'obs-emitter.c', \"obs-fields-$t.c\")\n252:         foreach ($unit in $units) {\n253:             $obj = Join-Path $objDir ([IO.Path]::ChangeExtension($unit, '.o'))\n254:             $unitIncludes = if ($unit -like 'obs-fields-*') { ($includes -join ' ') } else { '' }\n255:             $command = ('gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -O2 -g {0} {1} -c \"{2}\" -o \"{3}\"' -f $defines, $unitIncludes, (ConvertTo-ShellPath (Join-Path $wrappers $unit)), (ConvertTo-ShellPath $obj))\n256:             $cl = Invoke-Native -Command $command -WorkingDirectory $objDir -LogPath (Join-Path $receipts \"compile-$t-$([IO.Path]::GetFileNameWithoutExtension($unit)).log\")\n257:             if ($cl.ExitCode -ne 0) { throw \"wrapper compile failed for $unit ($t): $($cl.Stderr)\" }\n258:             $objects += (ConvertTo-ShellPath $obj)\n259:         }\n260:         $wrapSymbols = @($cycleSymbol, 'mem_initialize_memory', 'mem_mmu_translate', 'mem_toggle_watchpoints', 'vice_sharp_observe_clock')\n261:         if ($t -eq 'x64sc') { $wrapSymbols += 'vicii_steal_cycles' }\n262:         $wraps = $wrapSymbols | ForEach-Object { \"-Wl,--wrap=$_\" }\n263:         $output = Join-Path $bin (\"{0}-observer{1}\" -f $t, $exeExt)\n264:         $stock = [string]$linkLines.$t\n265:         $program = [regex]::Escape($t + $exeExt)\n266:         # The wrapper objects go directly after \"-o <output>\", ahead of every stock object and archive: ld pulls\n267:         # archive members only for references already seen, so __real_<symbol> (resolved to the stock symbol\n268:         # inside an archive) must be referenced before that archive is scanned. The --wrap flags can go last.\n269:         $quotedObjects = ($objects | ForEach-Object { '\"' + $_ + '\"' }) -join ' '\n270:         $relink = $stock -replace \"\\s-o\\s+$program(\\s|$)\", (' -o \"' + (ConvertTo-ShellPath $output) + '\" ' + $quotedObjects + '$1')\n271:         $relink = $relink + ' ' + ($wraps -join ' ')\n272:         $log = Join-Path $receipts \"link-$t.log\"\n273:         $r = Invoke-Native -Command $relink -WorkingDirectory (Join-Path $build 'src') -LogPath $log\n274:         if ($r.ExitCode -ne 0) { throw \"relink failed for $t (exit $($r.ExitCode)); see $log\" }\n275:         $nm = Invoke-Native -Command ('nm \"{0}\"' -f (ConvertTo-ShellPath $output)) -WorkingDirectory $bin -LogPath (Join-Path $receipts \"nm-$t.log\")\n276:         $wrapSymbols = @(($nm.Stdout -split \"`n\") | Where-Object { $_ -match '__wrap_|__real_|vice_sharp_' } | ForEach-Object { $_.Trim() })\n277:         $results += [pscustomobject]@{\n278:             Machine = $t; Output = $output; Sha256 = (Get-FileHash -LiteralPath $output -Algorithm SHA256).Hash\n279:             StockLinkLine = $stock; RelinkLine = $relink; WrapperObjects = $objects; Wraps = $wraps; WrapSymbols = $wrapSymbols\n280:         }\n281:         Write-Output (\"linked {0}: {1}\" -f $t, $output)\n282:     }\n283:     Write-Receipt -Name 'link' -Object ([pscustomobject]@{ Stage = 'Link'; Host = $hostTag; Results = $results }) | Out-Null\n284: }\n285: \n286: function Step-Inventory {\n287:     $listPath = Join-Path $receipts 'materialization-hashes.txt'\n288:     if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $listPath)) { throw \"Run the Copy stage first: $listPath missing\" }\n289:     $beforeMap = @{}\n290:     foreach ($line in Get-Content -LiteralPath $listPath) { $sha, $path = $line -split ' ', 2; $beforeMap[$path] = $sha }\n291:     $afterList = Get-TreeHashList -Root $source\n292:     $afterMap = @{}\n293:     foreach ($e in $afterList) { $afterMap[$e.Path] = $e.Sha256 }\n294:     $changed = @($beforeMap.Keys | Where-Object { $afterMap.ContainsKey($_) -and $afterMap[$_] -ne $beforeMap[$_] } | Sort-Object)\n295:     $removed = @($beforeMap.Keys | Where-Object { -not $afterMap.ContainsKey($_) } | Sort-Object)\n296:     $added =