al call. The guarantee: in the wrapped per-cycle hook, the wrapper rescans the rows the four live pointers resolve to (read, write, dummy read, dummy write; at most four rows of 0x101 entries) and re-patches, counting every entry found unpatched as a fail-closed `Missing` for that cycle, never a silent gap. A row rewritten while not current is covered by the same rescan because a row becomes current only through a pointer reassignment that precedes the next access by at least one hook, which is exactly what the ordering proof must establish for every path: banking (`mem_pla_config_changed`), the resource setters (`vic20mem.c` 396 to 462), the 256K paths (`c64memsc.c` 644 to 667), cartridge configuration and the watchpoint toggle; where a rewrite can precede an access inside the same cycle, that access is a fail-closed `Missing`, and if the proof shows it can happen on a workload the lockstep needs, that is a named blocker. The S0 audit lists every assignment site of the four pointers and every writer of the table contents per machine (direct array writes included), classified as cross-object (wrappable) or intra-object (rescan only), with the thread on which each runs.\n10: 4. Half-cycle and phase: the per-cycle hook (`__wrap_vic_cycle` for xvic; the `vicii_cycle` equivalent for x64sc, named by the nm probe) advances the half-cycle index and emits the `VICE_OBS` line of the step 1 contract once per CPU cycle with the accesses captured since the previous hook, PHI1 for the dummy-read-only or idle half and PHI2 for the access half as VICE's single-cycle model exposes them; where VICE performs no access in a cycle the kind is `idle` and `data` is what the contract requires on idle half-cycles (the step 1 self-assessment's open item, resolved by evidence in S3: either the last bus value VICE exposes or a fail-closed `Missing:Data`, never an invented value).\n11: 5. Interrupt mask and stolen cycles: `maincpu_int_status->global_pending_int` (reachable global per the nm pr