generated when MCP Server refreshes the marker.\n18: 2. Keep this file focused on durable ViceSharp workspace policy and conventions; avoid duplicating marker-file API keys, endpoints, or operational details that can rotate.\n19: 3. Use helper tools or plugins for session log, TODO, requirements, import/export, and traceability operations. Do not make raw REST calls, direct DB edits, or direct YAML state edits when a supported helper/tool exists.\n20: 4. Persist session-log updates immediately after each meaningful change: turn creation, action append, decision, requirement, blocker, file/context update, validation result, commit, or push.\n21: 5. Capture rich turn detail: interpretation, response, status, actions with type/status/filePath, contextList, filesModified, designDecisions, requirementsDiscovered, blockers, and relevant processing dialog.\n22: 6. Follow workspace conventions in this file, `README.md`, `docs/plan.md`, `HANDOFF.md`, and `.github/copilot-instructions.md`.\n23: 7. In Codex Desktop, use PowerShell.MCP `execute_command` for git, dotnet, Nuke, Bash, Node, PowerShell, and file-system commands. With the local `powershell` server key, the host-qualified tool is `mcp__powershell__execute_command`. Calling `start_console` first is optional because `execute_command` auto-starts or reuses a console; use `start_console` explicitly only to pre-warm at a location, show a banner, or force an additional console with `reason`.\n24: 8. If Bash is required from PowerShell.MCP, invoke explicit Git Bash at `C:\\Program Files\\Git\\bin\\bash.exe`; do not rely on bare `bash`.\n25: 9. Do not fabricate information. If you made a mistake, acknowledge it. Distinguish facts from speculation.\n26: 10. Prioritize correctness over speed. Do not ship code you have not verified compiles and is logically sound.\n27: 11. When writing session logs or other audit records, identify the real agent accurately using the correct Pascal-case source type. Do not use placeholder, legacy, or misleading agent identities.\n28: 12. Do not permanently delete workspace files unless the user explicitly asks for permanent deletion. Use the Windows recycle bin for user-requested deletions when possible.\n29: \n30: ## Where Things Live\n31: \n32: - `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` - MCP marker, current API key, endpoints, workspace config, and runtime instructions.\n33: - `AGENTS.md` - durable workspace policy for agents.\n34: - `ViceSharp.slnx` - main .NET 10 solution.\n35: - `src/` - production projects: core emulator, chip models, host/runtime services, Avalonia UI, protocol, launcher, ROM fetch, monitor, and platform hosts.\n36: - `tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/` - primary test harness and most behavioral/regression tests.\n37: - `tests/ViceSharp.Benchmarks/` - performance probes and benchmarks.\n38: - `tests/ViceSharp.AiReview.Tests/` - AI review harness tests.\n39: - `build/Build.cs` and `build.ps1` - Nuke build targets.\n40: - `HANDOFF.md` (repo root) - canonical handoff and recent session continuity notes.\n41: - `docs/plan.md` - historical/current plan context.\n42: - `docs/requirements/` - canonical FR/TR/TEST requirements, source manifests, and traceability artifacts.\n43: - `docs/Project/` - generated/exported project requirements and wiki mirrors.\n44: - `tools/check_requirement_traceability.ps1` - requirement ID traceability audit.\n45: - `tools/Publish-Wiki.ps1` - requirements wiki export/publish automation.\n46: - `TestResults/`, `validation-output/`, and `artifacts/` - retained evidence and generated build/test outputs.\n47: - `native/vice/` - upstream/native VICE source and validation reference material.\n48: \n49: ## Build and Test Commands\n50: \n51: Prefer PowerShell.MCP `execute_command` for all commands in Codex Desktop. Use `wait_for_completion` after a long-running command remains active, `cancel` for an interruptible runaway command, and `close_console` for an uninterruptible command or PowerShell host prompt.\n52: \n53: Common validation commands:\n54: \n55: ```pwsh\n56: dotnet build .\\ViceSharp.slnx\n57: dotnet test .\\ViceSharp.slnx\n58: dotnet test .\\tests\\ViceSharp.TestHarness\\ViceSharp.TestHarness.csproj -c Release --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~SomeFilter\"\n59: .\\tools\\check_requirement_traceability.ps1\n60: git diff --check\n61: ```\n62: \n63: Nuke targets:\n64: \n65: ```pwsh\n66: .\\build.ps1 Restore\n67: .\\build.ps1 Compile\n68: .\\build.ps1 Test\n69: .\\build.ps1 DeterminismTest\n70: .\\build.ps1 RunConsole\n71: .\\build.ps1 RunAvalonia\n72: .\\build.ps1 PublishWiki\n73: .\\build.ps1 PublishMsi\n74: .\\build.ps1 InstallMsi\n75: .\\build.ps1 PublishWinget\n76: .\\build.ps1 CiAzure\n77: ```\n78: \n79: Use the repo-supported Nuke targets for packaging and deployment. Do not invent a manual deployment path when `PublishMsi` or `InstallMsi` applies.\n80: \n81: ## Testing Evidence\n82: \n83: - Tests are evidence. Preserve enough output to prove what ran and what passed.\n84: - For focused test runs, record the exact command, filter, configuration, pass/fail/skip counts, and relevant output.\n85: - For full or long-running `dotnet test` runs, use retained evidence: stable `TestResults/` directory, TRX logger, VSTest diag log, captured stdout/stderr, and `--blame-hang` or dump collection when hangs are possible.\n86: - Do not claim a stopped, timed-out, or hung test process is a passing gate.\n87: - Do not count skipped tests as passing tests. If a run has skips, report the skip count and why it is acceptable for that scope.\n88: - Run `git diff --check` before claiming a code or docs slice is ready.\n89: \n90: ## Byrd Development Process\n91: \n92: - Use Byrd/BDPv4-style gated slices for implementation work.\n93: - Write or identify the failing test first when changing behavior.\n94: - Keep FR/TR/TEST IDs connected to implementation and tests.\n95: - Keep slices narrow enough to validate with focused tests before broader gates.\n96: - Do not leave deferred work as silent TODO comments or skipped tests. Put deferred work in MCP TODO/requirements state or an explicit handoff artifact.\n97: \n98: ## Architecture Guidance\n99: \n100: - ViceSharp is a C#/.NET 10 VICE-derived emulator. Preserve deterministic, cycle-aware emulator behavior.\n101: - Shared chip implementations must stay machine-agnostic. Machine/device glue belongs in Core machine/device adapters, not in generic chips.\n102: - The VIA chip must remain one common implementation; C1541/VIC-20-specific wiring belongs in the owning machine/device layer.\n103: - Preserve lockstep/checkpoint determinism when touching CPU, VIC-II, CIA, VIA, SID, IEC, storage, tape, input, snapshot, capture, or host pacing code.\n104: - Follow existing project conventions and public API XMLDocs. Public APIs and tests should document requirement/use-case/acceptance context where the local convention requires it.\n105: \n106: ## Source control and workflow\n107: \n108: GitHub `origin` (`https://github.com/sharpninja/vice-sharp.git`) is the source of truth. Azure DevOps is retired and is not used. Push and PR target `origin` only unless explicitly told otherwise. The default branch is `main`. Read `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml` and `HANDOFF.md` when resuming work; route TODO, session-log, and requirements operations through the MCP Server.\n109: \n110: ## MCP Interaction\n111: \n112: - Use the agent-appropriate MCP helper/plugin named or required by `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`.\n113: - For Codex, use the mcpserver-codex-plugin wrapper flow when available.\n114: - For hosted MCP agents, use dedicated tools such as session, TODO, requirements, repo, desktop, GitHub, and context tools when available.\n115: - Do not bypass supported helpers with raw REST calls to `/mcpserver/*` for session logs, TODOs, requirements, import/export, or traceability writes.\n116: - Raw REST is only a fallback for read-only diagnosis or schema inspection after the required helper/tool route is unavailable or insufficient.\n117: \n118: ## Context Loading by Task Type\n119: \n120: - Session startup or continuation: `AGENTS-README-FIRST.yaml`, then `docs/handoff.md`, then relevant MCP session/TODO state.\n121: - Requirements work: `docs/requirements/`, `docs/Project/Requirements-Matrix.md`, and `tools/check_requirement_traceability.ps1`.\n122: - Build/package/deploy: `README.md`, `build/Build.cs`, `build.ps1`, and current Nuke target behavior.\n123: - Emulator behavior: relevant `src/` implementation, `tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/`, `native/vice/`, and requirement IDs.\n124: - UI/host/debug surface: `src/ViceSharp.Avalonia/`, `src/ViceSharp.Host/`, `src/ViceSharp.Protocol/`, `tests/ViceSharp.TestHarness/Ui/`, and `docs/wireframes/` where applicable.\n125: - AI review work: inspect aiUnit config/resolver/CLI surfaces before changing bridge code.\n126: \n127: ## Agent Conduct\n128: \n129: You represent the workspace owner. Your work directly reflects the owner's professional reputation.\n130: \n131: ### Honesty\n132: \n133: - Do not fabricate information, capabilities, or results.\n134: - Distinguish between facts, informed opinions, and speculation.\n135: - Acknowledge mistakes immediately and correct them.\n136: \n137: ### Correctness\n138: \n139: - Prioritize correctness over speed.\n140: - When uncertain, state uncertainty and verify from live repo state.\n141: - Prefer proven local patterns over clever approaches unless directed otherwise.\n142: - Follow DRY, SOLID, and existing project conventions.\n143: - Do not ship code you have not verified compiles and is logically sound.\n144: \n145: ### Decision Documentation\n146: \n147: - Log design decisions to the session log.\n148: - For each decision, document what was decided, why, what alternatives were considered, and what was rejected.\n149: - Log design decisions as dialog entries with category `decision` and as session log actions with type `design_decision`.\n150: \n151: ### Professional Representation\n152: \n153: - Every interaction is audited through 