{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_76","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/pwsh -NoProfile -Command '$s = '\"'./docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Test-ReviewAssertions.ps1'; \"'$issues = @(Invoke-ScriptAnalyzer -Path $s -Severity Warning,Error); $issues; if ($issues.Count) { throw '\"'Analysis failed' }; & \"'$s -WhatIf; & $s; & '\"'./docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'README.md' -StartLine 1 -LineCount 100; & './docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol/Read-ReviewEvidence.ps1' -Operation Read -Paths 'docs/plan.md' -StartLine 1 -LineCount 70\"","aggregated_output":"What if: Performing the operation \"Write source-derived review assertions without running builds\" on target \"/home/sharpninja/github/vice-sharp/docs/receipts/hv/20261020T122200-plan-nativerestore-001-gpt61sol\".\n{\"nativeTrackedChanges\":8,\"nativeUntrackedEntries\":155,\"consumerFileCount\":31,\"harnessInvokesBuildNativeTarget\":true,\"oldUntrackedInventoryCount\":2527,\"builderSupportsWhatIf\":false,\"equivalenceRunnerSupportsWhatIf\":false,\"reviewerScriptFindings\":0}\nFILE: README.md (225 lines)\n1: # ViceSharp\n2: \n3: A C# port of [VICE](https://vice-emu.sourceforge.io/) (Versatile Commodore Emulator) targeting .NET 10.\n4: \n5: > **Parity is under active re-audit.** Native VICE is the immutable behavioral oracle. Historical C64 and VIC-20 lockstep receipts prove bounded slices only; they do not establish whole-emulator parity. Canonical native VICE `.vsf` save/load remains a required, open product gate. See [docs/Iteration-Roadmap.md](docs/Iteration-Roadmap.md), [PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001](docs/plans/PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001.md), and [PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001](docs/plans/PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001.md).\n6: \n7: ## Quick Start\n8: \n9: ```pwsh\n10: # 1. Clone and build\n11: git clone https://github.com/sharpninja/vice-sharp.git vice-sharp\n12: cd vice-sharp\n13: dotnet build ViceSharp.slnx\n14: \n15: # 2. Point at your VICE data root, or put x64sc.exe on PATH (see docs/ROMs.md)\n16: $env:VICESHARP_ROM_PATH = \"C:\\path\\to\\GTK3VICE-3.8-win64\"\n17: \n18: # 3. Boot a C64 with a true-drive 1541 attached\n19: dotnet run --project src/ViceSharp.Console -- `\n20:     --roms $env:VICESHARP_ROM_PATH `\n21:     --machine-yaml docs/samples/c64-plus-1541.multisystem.yaml `\n22:     --cycles 1000000\n23: ```\n24: \n25: Coming from classic VICE? The `ViceSharp.Launcher` library provides VICE-compatible argument parsing and topology generation for `x64`, `x64sc`, `xvic`, and `c1541`, including `-8`, `-9`, `-cart`, `+truedrive` / `-truedrive`. `ViceSharp.Console` still parses its process arguments as `x64sc`; use `--machine-yaml` for drive topologies because parsed disk/cartridge dispatch is not yet wired into the console. Standalone VICE-named binaries are not shipped. The generated `xvic` topology is not yet an end-to-end console path because the generic multi-system kind dispatcher recognizes only `C64` and `C1541`. See [docs/USER-GUIDE.md](docs/USER-GUIDE.md) and [docs/VICE-MIGRATION.md](docs/VICE-MIGRATION.md).\n26: \n27: ## Install\n28: \n29: The current line is **v1.3.3** (GitVersion `next-version`; NuGet + MSI / winget packaging). Published nuget.org / winget packages may lag until the next release tag.\n30: \n31: ```pwsh\n32: # Desktop UI as a dotnet global tool (command: vicesharp)\n33: dotnet tool install --global ViceSharp.Avalonia\n34: \n35: # Console reference shell as a dotnet global tool (command: vicesharp-console)\n36: dotnet tool install --global ViceSharp.Console\n37: \n38: # Embed the emulation core (Abstractions + Chips + RomFetch + Core + Architectures) in your own app\n39: dotnet add package ViceSharp.Core\n40: ```\n41: \n42: Individual packages (`ViceSharp.Protocol`, `ViceSharp.Monitor`, `ViceSharp.Launcher`, `ViceSharp.AdhocHelper`, `ViceSharp.Host`, `ViceSharp.SourceGen`, and the `ViceSharp.Host.MacOS` / `Android` / `iOS` shells) are published alongside the bundle. Xbox/UWP is outside the supported product scope; its source remains frozen legacy. The Windows desktop app is also packaged as a self-contained MSI (Nuke `PublishMsi`) with winget metadata (`PublishWinget`, package id `sharpninja.ViceSharp`).\n43: \n44: ### Linux: install for your user\n45: \n46: Run Nuke `DeployUser` as your regular user on Linux with a .NET SDK compatible with [global.json](global.json) (currently 10.0.301 or a later 10.0 feature band). It publishes a self-contained Release build for `x86_64` or `aarch64` and installs the app, launcher, desktop entry, and icon inside your home directory.\n47: \n48: ```bash\n49: ./build.sh DeployUser\n50: ~/.local/bin/vicesharp\n51: ```\n52: \n53: The PowerShell equivalent is `./build.ps1 DeployUser`. `DeployYay` is a compatibility alias for the same user-local deployment; it does not invoke `yay`, `pacman`, or `sudo`.\n54: \n55: Application releases live under `~/.local/share/vicesharp/releases/<version>-<unique>/`; the `current` symlink selects the active release. The launcher is `~/.local/bin/vicesharp`, and the desktop entry is `~/.local/share/applications/vicesharp.desktop`. If `XDG_DATA_HOME` is set, it replaces `~/.local/share` and must be an absolute path inside your home. Installation rejects root execution and symlinked or unmanaged destination conflicts. Older releases and existing `ViceSharp` user data are preserved.\n56: \n57: An existing system package remains installed. Put `~/.local/bin` before `/usr/bin` in your shell's `PATH` to select the user deployment with `vicesharp`; check with `command -v vicesharp`, or invoke `~/.local/bin/vicesharp` directly. The desktop entry selects the absolute user launcher independently of shell `PATH`.\n58: \n59: The app includes C64 GTK3 and SDL keyboard layouts under `data/C64` in its release directory. Input settings discover these `.vkm` files automatically, after configured VICE data directories and per-user keymaps. No system .NET SDK or runtime is required to run the installed app.\n60: \n61: Saving RomM credentials on Linux requires the optional `libsecret` package (which provides `secret-tool`) and a running, unlocked Secret Service keyring, such as GNOME Keyring or KWallet with Secret Service support enabled.\n62: \n63: Linux audio uses the SDL2 shared library (`libSDL2-2.0.so.0`, also provided by Arch's `sdl2-compat`) and the current user's audio service/device. `DeployUser` does not install system dependencies. The desktop enables sound by default; `VICESHARP_AUDIO=0` disables it. Headless and test hosts remain silent unless explicitly enabled. The C64 BASIC READY screen is normally silent; use a program that produces sound to check playback.\n64: \n65: On first local desktop launch, missing default C64 ROMs are downloaded from the VICE GitHub mirror, hash-verified, and cached under `~/.local/share/ViceSharp/roms` (or `$XDG_DATA_HOME/ViceSharp/roms`). Set `VICESHARP_ROM_PATH` or `VICE_DATA_PATH` to prefer an existing ROM set; see [docs/ROMs.md](docs/ROMs.md). ROMs are not bundled. See the [RomM library guide](docs/USER-GUIDE.md#6c-romm-library) for connecting a server and attaching downloaded games, including T64 archives.\n66: \n67: ### Optional Arch package build\n68: \n69: `./build.sh PackYay` (or `./build.ps1 PackYay`) remains a package-only target for explicit system-package workflows. It requires Arch Linux `base-devel`/`makepkg` and the native dependencies listed in the generated `PKGBUILD`; it does not install anything. Each build leaves `PKGBUILD`, `.SRCINFO`, a SHA256-pinned payload, and `vicesharp-<version>-1-<arch>.pkg.tar.zst` under `artifacts/yay/<version>/<arch>/`. Installing that archive is a separate system-administration action. Use `DeployUser` for user-local installation.\n70: \n71: Both targets accept `--package-version-override X.Y.Z`; when omitted, they use the release tag on HEAD or the GitVersion-derived default.\n72: \n73: ## User documentation\n74: \n75: - [docs/USER-GUIDE.md](docs/USER-GUIDE.md) - install, first run, CLI launcher, YAML topology, disk images, RomM library, desktop drag-drop (including `*.prg` load/RUN), capture, diagnostics attach, what works today\n76: - [docs/VICE-MIGRATION.md](docs/VICE-MIGRATION.md) - binary + flag mapping, behaviour caveats, performance / accuracy, bug compatibility\n77: - [docs/ROMs.md](docs/ROMs.md) - legal ROM options, environment variable, directory layout\n78: - [docs/](docs/README.md) - full documentation index (architecture, public API, iteration plans, diagrams)\n79: \n80: ## Status\n81: \n82: > Status audited 2026-10-04. Native VICE is the immutable behavioral oracle. Historical lockstep receipts remain valid only for the exact models, checkpoints, cycle budgets, and fields they name. They do not establish whole-machine or release-wide parity.\n83: \n84: - **C64:** Bounded x64sc lockstep and checkpoint suites exist, including historical 100,000-cycle and multi-frame receipts. Full classic VICE behavior and edge-case parity are being re-audited against active FR/TR/TEST acceptance criteria.\n85: - **VIC-20:** Historical PAL and NTSC 10-second A/X/Y/S/P/PC receipts exist, plus scoped video, cartridge, flash, and audio tests. Full xvic parity is not yet accepted. Follow [PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001](docs/plans/PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001.md) and [PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001](docs/plans/PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001.md).\n86: - **Snapshots:** The current host routes C64 snapshots through `vice-sharp.machine-snapshot.v2` and non-C64 snapshots through `vice-sharp.runtime-snapshot.v1`. Managed VSF staging already exists in the test harness, but normal public and desktop save/load paths do not yet use canonical native VICE `.vsf`. Proprietary formats are not a substitute for the required VICE format.\n87: - **Native boundary:** The checked-in `native/vice` revision is the immutable reference. Remediation moves build, observation, stepping, compatibility, and snapshot integration into external wrappers. No parity result may depend on modifying native VICE.\n88: - **VIC-20 settings:** The current Avalonia working tree binds VIC-20 memory and expansion sections to `IsVic20Selected`, and the ViewModel raises visibility changes when the selected profile changes or settings load.\n89: - **Product scope:** Avalonia desktop and Console are supported product shells. Microsoft Store and Xbox/UWP are cancelled legacy source, not current targets.\n90: \n91: ## Verification evidence\n92: \n93: Use exact receipts and acceptance tests, not percentage dashboards, to judge parity. Durable historical VIC-20 receipts are indexed in [docs/README.md](docs/README.md#lockstep-receipts-durable). Current remediation gates and their required zero-failed, zero-skipped evidence are defined in the two plans above.\n94: \n95: The 2026-10-04 traceability audit found 235 canonical IDs, 149 referenced in source/tests, 86 canonical IDs without source/test references, and 1,011 noncanonical IDs in source/tests. This is an open remediation baseline, not a green gate.\n96: \n97: ## Machine roadmap\n98: \n99: | Machine | Architecture | Status |\n100: |---------|-------------|--------|\nFILE: docs/plan.md (479 lines)\n1: # ViceSharp Phase 1 Completion Plan\n2: \n3: **HISTORICAL STATUS ONLY:** This file records the Phase 1 closeout claim made on 2026-05-31 at HEAD `6086e11` (1641 passed, 2 skipped, 0 failed). That claim and its skipped tests are not current acceptance evidence and do not establish whole-machine parity. For current continuity use root [HANDOFF.md](../HANDOFF.md); for current gates use [PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001](plans/PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001.md) and [PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001](plans/PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001.md).\n4: \n5: Updated: 2026-05-27 (audit refresh at HEAD 064d3a0; see handoff.md for full reconciliation)\n6: Workspace: `F:\\GitHub\\vice-sharp`\n7: Baseline: `main` at `064d3a0` (post-audit; prior 2026-05-21 snapshot at 46edda9 / 8c1b2fc)\n8: \n9: This file is retained as a historical Phase 1 closeout record. It is not the live plan and must not override current requirements, the canonical handoff, or the two current remediation plans.\n10: \n11: ## Sources Of Truth\n12: \n13: For current work, this historical file yields to these sources:\n14: \n15: 1. Primary MCP requirements, TODO, and session state after supported plugin readback.\n16: 2. `docs/plans/PLAN-VICE-IMMUTABLE-001.md` and `docs/plans/PLAN-VSFLOCKSTEP-001.md`.\n17: 3. Root `HANDOFF.md` for continuity.\n18: 4. Current code, tests, retained validation evidence, and git state.\n19: 5. This file and other archived plans only as historical context.\n20: \n21: Slice 0 reconciliation is now the baseline for this plan. It used live MCP TODO\n22: state, subagent code/test review, and focused validation to separate stale open\n23: TODOs from real Phase 1 blockers before new feature work continues.\n24: \n25: ## Phase 1 Definition\n26: \n27: In this historical record, Phase 1 meant a C64 bringup sufficient to operate selected x64sc profiles through boot, input, media, capture, snapshot, and host-control flows with the validation then available. It also targeted at least 25% of classic VICE performance on one selected benchmark profile. Those historical bounds did not mean every VICE behavior, machine, cartridge mapper, platform host, or current parity gate was complete.\n28: \n29: The historical 2026 closeout criteria were:\n30: \n31: 1. Every Phase 1 implementation slice is driven by current canonical\n32:    `FR-*`, `TR-*`, and `TEST-*` requirements, with VICE source evidence cited\n33:    before code changes. If VICE source or classic documentation proves a\n34:    requirement is incomplete or wrong, the requirement is corrected before the\n35:    implementation is treated as done.\n36: 2. C64 and required x64sc profiles boot to BASIC `READY.` with ROM-backed reset\n37:    and deterministic managed/native validation still green.\n38: 3. CPU, 6510 port, PLA, memory map, ROM loader, CIA, SID MVP, VIA, interrupt,\n39:    and reset behavior remain closed under the normal full-suite gate.\n40: 4. VIC-II MVP parity includes deeper visible-frame validation: badlines, DMA CPU\n41:    inhibition, sprite DMA, sprite priority and collisions, borders/open borders,\n42:    bank switching effects, raster IRQ behavior, and model timing checkpoints.\n43: 5. Media MVP works from the runtime and host surfaces: true-drive 1541/D64\n44:    attach and load path, TAP datasette motor/timing path, standard cartridge\n45:    live memory mapping and boot behavior, full-machine snapshot resume, and\n46:    deterministic frame/audio capture.\n47: 6. Keyboard, joystick/control-port, and host/UI control flows are usable through\n48:    the gRPC boundary without bypassing the host-owned emulator lifecycle.\n49: 7. Final x64sc lockstep gates run without unsupported required-profile skips.\n50:    Missing native VICE binaries or configured VICE data roots count as\n51:    blockers, not passes.\n52: 8. A minimal upstream VICE testbench path exists for selected x64sc-compatible\n53:    cases, including debugcart exit handling and process-level smoke validation.\n54: 9. `PERF-TUNING-001` is complete: the selected classic VICE comparison profile\n55:    has a repeatable benchmark, the top measured vice-sharp hotspots have been\n56:    profiled and remediated, and vice-sharp reaches at least 25% of classic VICE\n57:    performance on that profile without breaking deterministic validation.\n58: 10. MCP TODO, session log, README dashboard, `handoff.md`, and this file all\n59:    agree on what is done, what is deferred, and what remains post-Phase 1.\n60: \n61: ## Byrd Slice Rules\n62: \n63: Every implementation slice follows the Byrd Development Process:\n64: \n65: 1. Open/update the MCP session turn and query the relevant TODOs.\n66: 2. Identify the exact canonical `FR-*`, `TR-*`, and `TEST-*` IDs, plus the\n67:    VICE source files that define the behavior. Broad labels such as `FR-VIC`\n68:    are audit findings, not sufficient slice anchors.\n69: 3. Correct requirement text, traceability maps, or test requirements first when\n70:    the imported VICE requirement is incomplete, stale, or contradicted by VICE\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
